And you might want to look at the first question of problem

20
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:00,570
set two if you happen to have time before Thursday's lecture,

21
00:01:00,570 --> 00:01:03,630
because it can help motivate-- you don't have to solve it,

22
00:01:03,630 --> 00:01:05,610
but it can help motivate and put into context

23
00:01:05,610 --> 00:01:07,130
what we'll see on Thursday.

24
00:01:07,130 --> 00:01:10,770
So I'd recommend taking a look at that first problem.

25
00:01:10,770 --> 00:01:12,570
And then you'll kind of know what

26
00:01:12,570 --> 00:01:15,530
to look out for in the lecture to help you solve that problem.

27
00:01:15,530 --> 00:01:18,710
So just a little piece of advice.

28
00:01:18,710 --> 00:01:19,710
And I think that's it.

29
00:01:19,710 --> 00:01:21,210
So we're going to go right in.

30
00:01:21,210 --> 00:01:24,870
So the outline for today will be, what is generalization?

31
00:01:24,870 --> 00:01:25,970
That should be review.

32
00:01:25,970 --> 00:01:28,530
You've all taken machine learning or classes like this

33
00:01:28,530 --> 00:01:30,390
before.

34
00:01:30,390 --> 00:01:34,410
Then, the next question is, do deep nets generalize?

35
00:01:34,410 --> 00:01:36,650
Then, we'll see that there's some classical theories

36
00:01:36,650 --> 00:01:39,450
of generalization, and they don't actually work very well

37
00:01:39,450 --> 00:01:40,630
for deep learning.

38
00:01:40,630 --> 00:01:43,850
So we'll see some failures of classical generalization theory

39
00:01:43,850 --> 00:01:46,970
at explaining phenomena in deep learning.

40
00:01:46,970 --> 00:01:49,210
And then I've already given away the answer--

41
00:01:49,210 --> 00:01:52,070
that, to some degree, deep nets, will argue, do generalize.

42
00:01:52,070 --> 00:01:54,370
So why do they generalize?

43
00:01:54,370 --> 00:01:57,830
What does this tell us about their inductive biases

44
00:01:57,830 --> 00:02:01,750
and preferences toward solutions that will generalize over

45
00:02:01,750 --> 00:02:06,590
solutions that simply fit the data but don't generalize?

46
00:02:06,590 --> 00:02:08,830
So that's the outline.

47
00:02:08,830 --> 00:02:12,870
And I should also say that the material in this lecture

48
00:02:12,870 --> 00:02:14,950
is not really textbook material.

49
00:02:14,950 --> 00:02:17,450
We gave a few readings, but there are kind of recent papers,

50
00:02:17,450 --> 00:02:19,870
so we just listed them as optional.

51
00:02:19,870 --> 00:02:22,950
This is very much ongoing science.

52
00:02:22,950 --> 00:02:25,590
This is, to me, maybe kind of the biggest,

53
00:02:25,590 --> 00:02:28,590
or one of the biggest open sets of questions

54
00:02:28,590 --> 00:02:31,390
of topics of inquiry in the field of deep learning

55
00:02:31,390 --> 00:02:34,790
and in AI in general, is exactly what are the generalization

56
00:02:34,790 --> 00:02:36,348
properties of deep networks?

57
00:02:36,348 --> 00:02:38,390
They seem to work a lot better than we would have

58
00:02:38,390 --> 00:02:40,830
expected from classical theory.

59
00:02:40,830 --> 00:02:41,370
So why?

60
00:02:41,370 --> 00:02:42,410
We don't actually know.

61
00:02:42,410 --> 00:02:43,410
We don't have all the answers.

62
00:02:43,410 --> 00:02:45,160
This lecture will just pose some questions

63
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:47,970
and give some initial threads that you could pull on.

64
00:02:47,970 --> 00:02:49,470
But this is going to be a topic that

65
00:02:49,470 --> 00:02:51,345
will change a lot over the next coming years.

66
00:02:51,345 --> 00:02:54,650
I think this will develop a lot.

67
00:02:54,650 --> 00:02:57,160
So let's go over the basics of approximation

68
00:02:57,160 --> 00:02:58,300
versus generalization.

69
00:02:58,300 --> 00:03:00,280
We've so far talked in this course really only

70
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:01,920
about approximation.

71
00:03:01,920 --> 00:03:04,900
So approximation is, I have some training data.

72
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:07,880
I want to ask how well can I fit that training data.

73
00:03:07,880 --> 00:03:11,000
And we measure the fit with the empirical risk, which

74
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,020
is the first equation up here.

75
00:03:13,020 --> 00:03:15,440
So we just say, what is the loss L

76
00:03:15,440 --> 00:03:19,092
incurred over all of my training data points?

77
00:03:19,092 --> 00:03:20,800
This is a supervised setting where I have

78
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:23,060
data points x and outputs y.

79
00:03:23,060 --> 00:03:26,020
I'm trying to predict the correct output y for a given x.

80
00:03:26,020 --> 00:03:28,120
So I just sum up all of my prediction errors

81
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:30,240
according to my model f and my loss

82
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:34,200
function L. So this is just, on average,

83
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:37,040
what is my prediction error.

84
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:39,480
And we had a few lectures, and in the problems

85
00:03:39,480 --> 00:03:41,120
that you worked out, cases where you

86
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:46,600
can find that class of functions can minimize that towards 0

87
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:50,480
with sufficient capacity, with sufficient width or depth.

88
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:53,220
But that's not actually what we care about in machine learning.

89
00:03:53,220 --> 00:03:56,530
All we actually care about is the test error,

90
00:03:56,530 --> 00:03:59,090
which can be called the population risk.

91
00:03:59,090 --> 00:04:03,150
So the population risk is, on new samples from the world,

92
00:04:03,150 --> 00:04:05,970
new samples from the data generating process p.

93
00:04:05,970 --> 00:04:08,790
So new samples, x, y, new observations,

94
00:04:08,790 --> 00:04:11,410
how well do I do in expectation?

95
00:04:11,410 --> 00:04:13,570
And this is like the population, because it's

96
00:04:13,570 --> 00:04:17,610
over the entire population of observations

97
00:04:17,610 --> 00:04:21,370
I could get from my world's data generating process p.

98
00:04:21,370 --> 00:04:24,430
But you could also have an empirical test risk,

99
00:04:24,430 --> 00:04:26,090
which would just be over a sample,

100
00:04:26,090 --> 00:04:28,310
like my test set is a sample of 10,000 images--

101
00:04:28,310 --> 00:04:30,170
how well do I do on them?

102
00:04:30,170 --> 00:04:34,330
So we have three different kind of topics

103
00:04:34,330 --> 00:04:37,630
that we can factorize machine learning into.

104
00:04:37,630 --> 00:04:41,670
One is, how well can you approximate the training data?

105
00:04:41,670 --> 00:04:44,290
And that's what we've talked about so far.

106
00:04:44,290 --> 00:04:48,570
In my family of functions I'm considering

107
00:04:48,570 --> 00:04:52,980
parameterized by theta, what is the best theta

108
00:04:52,980 --> 00:04:56,140
star that will achieve the lowest empirical risk?

109
00:04:56,140 --> 00:04:58,780
And we've said that you can get it to go

110
00:04:58,780 --> 00:05:01,420
to 0 under some conditions.

111
00:05:01,420 --> 00:05:03,480
So we can fit our training data.

112
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:07,200
But then the next question is optimization.

113
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:09,012
How do we actually find that theta star?

114
00:05:09,012 --> 00:05:10,720
And we've talked a little bit about that.

115
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:11,600
We saw backprop.

116
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:15,500
And on Thursday's lecture, we'll talk more about optimization.

117
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:19,332
But we said that you can often find theta star to some degree.

118
00:05:19,332 --> 00:05:21,040
And this lecture is, well, wait a second.

119
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:24,340
If you find that really good parameter vector on the training

120
00:05:24,340 --> 00:05:26,560
data, what is the population risk?

121
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:29,180
What is the risk on the test data?

122
00:05:29,180 --> 00:05:32,060
So what is the gap between the training error and the test

123
00:05:32,060 --> 00:05:32,560
error?

124
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:34,000
And that's the question of generalization.

125
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:35,542
And that's all we actually care about

126
00:05:35,542 --> 00:05:37,140
because we want to do well when we

127
00:05:37,140 --> 00:05:40,100
deploy the system in the world and we see new observations.

128
00:05:40,100 --> 00:05:41,220
This should be review.

129
00:05:41,220 --> 00:05:45,140
This is like ML 101.

130
00:05:45,140 --> 00:05:46,320
Yeah, question?

131
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:48,100
AUDIENCE: If the r in approximations

132
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:49,840
supposed to have a hat on it, it's

133
00:05:49,840 --> 00:05:51,900
the question about training data?

134
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:55,455
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, I think you could ask the question--

135
00:05:55,455 --> 00:05:57,580
so an approximation-- yeah, typically, it would be,

136
00:05:57,580 --> 00:06:00,740
can I minimize the empirical risk on the training data?

137
00:06:00,740 --> 00:06:02,880
Yes.

138
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:05,280
Oh, I see.

139
00:06:05,280 --> 00:06:09,160
Yeah, we could have denoted that with a hat.

140
00:06:09,160 --> 00:06:12,400
But I suppose you could also ask the question,

141
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:15,880
is the best possible function in the hypothesis space

142
00:06:15,880 --> 00:06:20,473
going to achieve low risk on the population as well?

143
00:06:20,473 --> 00:06:23,140
So you could ask-- both of those are questions of approximation.

144
00:06:30,620 --> 00:06:35,480
So let's go over some intuitive ideas about generalization just

145
00:06:35,480 --> 00:06:40,160
to get warmed up and get you thinking about this.

146
00:06:40,160 --> 00:06:42,933
So here's a bad data set.

147
00:06:42,933 --> 00:06:44,600
Suppose we have a data set which is just

148
00:06:44,600 --> 00:06:47,280
a bunch of photos of cats, and we

149
00:06:47,280 --> 00:06:50,090
want to train a cat versus dog classifier.

150
00:06:50,090 --> 00:06:52,710
So clearly, this is not going to work.

151
00:06:52,710 --> 00:06:53,790
You only observe cats.

152
00:06:53,790 --> 00:06:56,130
You're not going to get a cat versus dog classifier.

153
00:06:56,130 --> 00:06:58,832
So what's wrong with this data?

154
00:06:58,832 --> 00:07:00,290
What would make for better training

155
00:07:00,290 --> 00:07:04,290
data for a cat versus dog classifier?

156
00:07:04,290 --> 00:07:05,390
Some obvious ideas.

157
00:07:05,390 --> 00:07:05,890
Yeah?

158
00:07:05,890 --> 00:07:06,980
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

159
00:07:06,980 --> 00:07:08,730
PHILLIP ISOLA: You want to have some dogs.

160
00:07:08,730 --> 00:07:09,950
Yeah, what else?

161
00:07:09,950 --> 00:07:12,250
What if you have just one cat, one dog, and then a lot

162
00:07:12,250 --> 00:07:15,950
of copies of that image?

163
00:07:15,950 --> 00:07:16,830
You want diversity.

164
00:07:16,830 --> 00:07:17,710
You want coverage.

165
00:07:17,710 --> 00:07:19,270
You want to show all your different conditions.

166
00:07:19,270 --> 00:07:21,510
So there are certain properties of the data that are critical.

167
00:07:21,510 --> 00:07:23,260
You can't really understand generalization

168
00:07:23,260 --> 00:07:25,410
without understanding the data distribution

169
00:07:25,410 --> 00:07:26,730
that you're training on.

170
00:07:26,730 --> 00:07:29,050
And if your training set doesn't represent

171
00:07:29,050 --> 00:07:30,770
all the interesting aspects of variation

172
00:07:30,770 --> 00:07:32,110
that you'll encounter in the world,

173
00:07:32,110 --> 00:07:33,830
then you're not going to generalize well.

174
00:07:33,830 --> 00:07:36,830
So data is critical to generalization.

175
00:07:36,830 --> 00:07:40,290
And we have to understand data to understand generalization.

176
00:07:40,290 --> 00:07:44,170
The other thing that's critical to understanding generalization

177
00:07:44,170 --> 00:07:46,640
is the model, which will be most of what we focus on.

178
00:07:46,640 --> 00:07:48,890
I just wanted to start by saying that the data is also

179
00:07:48,890 --> 00:07:52,880
a critical part of the recipe for generalization.

180
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:56,500
So here's a bad model--

181
00:07:56,500 --> 00:07:58,060
the filing cabinet.

182
00:07:58,060 --> 00:08:00,300
So the filing cabinet model is going

183
00:08:00,300 --> 00:08:02,500
to be this little Python program here,

184
00:08:02,500 --> 00:08:04,760
where we have a dictionary called cabinet.

185
00:08:04,760 --> 00:08:09,220
And if we see a new data point x, we put it into our cabinet

186
00:08:09,220 --> 00:08:13,060
along with the correct prediction y for that x.

187
00:08:13,060 --> 00:08:14,700
And if we come across that x again,

188
00:08:14,700 --> 00:08:16,755
we just hash into that cabinet.

189
00:08:16,755 --> 00:08:17,880
We just pull out that file.

190
00:08:17,880 --> 00:08:19,460
We see what the answer is.

191
00:08:19,460 --> 00:08:21,520
So this is sometimes called memorization.

192
00:08:21,520 --> 00:08:23,620
I'm just memorizing my training data.

193
00:08:23,620 --> 00:08:26,300
But the questions for you are, well,

194
00:08:26,300 --> 00:08:28,860
what will the approximation error be of the filing cabinet

195
00:08:28,860 --> 00:08:30,280
model?

196
00:08:30,280 --> 00:08:30,780
Yeah?

197
00:08:30,780 --> 00:08:31,420
AUDIENCE: 0.

198
00:08:31,420 --> 00:08:32,462
PHILLIP ISOLA: 0-- right.

199
00:08:32,462 --> 00:08:34,480
So the approximation error is zero

200
00:08:34,480 --> 00:08:38,059
because for every data point that I train on,

201
00:08:38,059 --> 00:08:40,980
I can retrieve the exact memorized answer.

202
00:08:40,980 --> 00:08:42,520
What about the generalization error?

203
00:08:42,520 --> 00:08:45,817
What will the generalization error of the filing cabinet be?

204
00:08:45,817 --> 00:08:47,400
Generalization error is the difference

205
00:08:47,400 --> 00:08:50,020
between the empirical risk and the population risk.

206
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:53,880
So roughly, what will it be?

207
00:08:53,880 --> 00:08:55,800
Will it be good, will it be bad?

208
00:08:55,800 --> 00:08:56,520
AUDIENCE: Bad.

209
00:08:56,520 --> 00:08:57,312
PHILLIP ISOLA: Bad.

210
00:08:57,312 --> 00:08:59,560
Yeah, it'll generally be bad unless it happens

211
00:08:59,560 --> 00:09:02,320
to be that the true function we're modeling

212
00:09:02,320 --> 00:09:04,000
is 0 almost everywhere, which would

213
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:05,680
be a very strange function.

214
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:06,920
Because we said by--

215
00:09:06,920 --> 00:09:09,560
if I don't have my file in the cabinet,

216
00:09:09,560 --> 00:09:12,020
I will just not know what to do.

217
00:09:12,020 --> 00:09:15,175
But maybe I got lucky, and the true function is 0 everywhere.

218
00:09:15,175 --> 00:09:16,800
So you could have a filing cabinet that

219
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:18,160
gets low generalization error, but it

220
00:09:18,160 --> 00:09:20,160
would be a very strange function that you're

221
00:09:20,160 --> 00:09:23,080
modeling in that case.

222
00:09:23,080 --> 00:09:26,060
So we'll come back to the filing cabinet model.

223
00:09:26,060 --> 00:09:29,160
And one of the questions is, are deep nets like filing cabinets

224
00:09:29,160 --> 00:09:31,772
or something else?

225
00:09:31,772 --> 00:09:33,480
Do they just memorize and not generalize,

226
00:09:33,480 --> 00:09:34,780
or do they do something else?

227
00:09:37,600 --> 00:09:40,920
Here's another bad model just, I guess, a little bit for fun.

228
00:09:40,920 --> 00:09:43,030
So this is Paul the Octopus.

229
00:09:43,030 --> 00:09:45,130
And Paul can decide who's going to win

230
00:09:45,130 --> 00:09:49,330
a soccer match between two different countries.

231
00:09:49,330 --> 00:09:53,090
And Paul was super accurate, and people would make their bets

232
00:09:53,090 --> 00:09:54,230
based on what Paul said.

233
00:09:54,230 --> 00:09:57,930
And what Paul did is just went into the tank of the flag

234
00:09:57,930 --> 00:09:59,990
that it thought would win the match.

235
00:09:59,990 --> 00:10:03,520
And it was like it got 6 out of 6 correct predictions.

236
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:05,770
So would you trust Paul to make the correct prediction

237
00:10:05,770 --> 00:10:07,770
next year?

238
00:10:07,770 --> 00:10:11,205
Yeah, it's probably like some super intelligent octopus.

239
00:10:11,205 --> 00:10:12,830
No, obviously, you wouldn't trust Paul.

240
00:10:12,830 --> 00:10:15,970
It must have just generalized-- sorry,

241
00:10:15,970 --> 00:10:17,350
it fit the training data well.

242
00:10:17,350 --> 00:10:18,510
It's not a filing cabinet.

243
00:10:18,510 --> 00:10:21,750
It is making some decision, but it happened to get lucky.

244
00:10:21,750 --> 00:10:23,370
It's like some random function that

245
00:10:23,370 --> 00:10:24,787
happened to fit the training data,

246
00:10:24,787 --> 00:10:26,410
but you'd never expect it to actually

247
00:10:26,410 --> 00:10:30,570
have a true model of soccer.

248
00:10:30,570 --> 00:10:33,610
So you can have functions that get lucky

249
00:10:33,610 --> 00:10:38,610
and make interesting predictions out of sample on the test data,

250
00:10:38,610 --> 00:10:40,190
but wrong predictions.

251
00:10:40,190 --> 00:10:42,940
And Paul is an example.

252
00:10:42,940 --> 00:10:45,860
So our big question in this course, in this lecture,

253
00:10:45,860 --> 00:10:48,980
is do deep nets generalize?

254
00:10:48,980 --> 00:10:52,660
So here's a simple warm-up.

255
00:10:52,660 --> 00:10:56,760
Here's the filing cabinet fit to these black data points.

256
00:10:56,760 --> 00:11:00,060
So my training data is the black points.

257
00:11:00,060 --> 00:11:01,500
It's a scalar to scalar mapping.

258
00:11:01,500 --> 00:11:03,700
I'm predicting y given x.

259
00:11:03,700 --> 00:11:07,160
And the red line is the fit of my model.

260
00:11:07,160 --> 00:11:10,700
And if my model is a filing cabinet, what it's saying

261
00:11:10,700 --> 00:11:15,340
is that I've memorized that if I see one of my training data

262
00:11:15,340 --> 00:11:17,050
points, I know exactly what to predict.

263
00:11:17,050 --> 00:11:19,300
That's the kind of red lines, like the delta functions

264
00:11:19,300 --> 00:11:22,380
going down to each of those dots, or up to the dots.

265
00:11:22,380 --> 00:11:23,960
And everywhere else, I'll predict 0.

266
00:11:23,960 --> 00:11:27,880
So anything that is not anything on the x-axis--

267
00:11:27,880 --> 00:11:30,820
any point I could sample on the x-axis that's not in my training

268
00:11:30,820 --> 00:11:34,620
set that's not where the black dots lie, I will predict 0 for.

269
00:11:34,620 --> 00:11:38,180
So this is a very bad kind of generalization.

270
00:11:38,180 --> 00:11:40,270
So here's the same data, where I've just fit it

271
00:11:40,270 --> 00:11:43,890
with a three layer ReLU MLP.

272
00:11:43,890 --> 00:11:45,230
Not too surprising.

273
00:11:45,230 --> 00:11:47,510
A ReLU MLP can fit data, but notice that it fits it

274
00:11:47,510 --> 00:11:49,070
in a very different way.

275
00:11:49,070 --> 00:11:51,303
So both of these achieve 0 approximation error.

276
00:11:51,303 --> 00:11:52,970
Both of them have fit the training data.

277
00:11:52,970 --> 00:11:54,428
But if that were all that mattered,

278
00:11:54,428 --> 00:11:56,637
then we would have to say these are equally good.

279
00:11:56,637 --> 00:11:57,970
But that's not all that matters.

280
00:11:57,970 --> 00:12:00,750
It also matters how you interpolate and extrapolate

281
00:12:00,750 --> 00:12:02,030
from the training data.

282
00:12:02,030 --> 00:12:03,790
And deep nets do that in a way that's

283
00:12:03,790 --> 00:12:07,510
markedly different than a filing cabinet.

284
00:12:07,510 --> 00:12:10,850
So memorization is just, what do I predict on the training data

285
00:12:10,850 --> 00:12:11,350
points?

286
00:12:11,350 --> 00:12:13,050
And filing cabinets can memorize--

287
00:12:13,050 --> 00:12:14,270
deep nets can memorize.

288
00:12:14,270 --> 00:12:16,830
And generalization is, what do I do on the points

289
00:12:16,830 --> 00:12:18,430
where I didn't have training data?

290
00:12:18,430 --> 00:12:21,930
And we might expect that this type of interpolation,

291
00:12:21,930 --> 00:12:24,890
this type of generalization is better than that other type,

292
00:12:24,890 --> 00:12:26,630
because most functions in the world

293
00:12:26,630 --> 00:12:31,702
are going to be smooth as opposed to spiky.

294
00:12:31,702 --> 00:12:34,410
You could imagine an adversarial world where that's not the case,

295
00:12:34,410 --> 00:12:37,710
but our world looks more like this on the right.

296
00:12:41,990 --> 00:12:43,910
So that's a very simple deep net.

297
00:12:43,910 --> 00:12:45,810
But the real feats of generalization

298
00:12:45,810 --> 00:12:47,570
that we get excited about these days

299
00:12:47,570 --> 00:12:51,250
are with fancy, big modern nets.

300
00:12:51,250 --> 00:12:55,670
So Here's a little experiment you can do.

301
00:12:55,670 --> 00:12:58,818
I just ran this for myself this weekend,

302
00:12:58,818 --> 00:13:00,610
but I think it might be a fun thing for you

303
00:13:00,610 --> 00:13:03,030
to play with or maybe take into a final project.

304
00:13:03,030 --> 00:13:07,970
So let's try to ask, are modern deep nets--

305
00:13:07,970 --> 00:13:10,148
the best model you can think of--

306
00:13:10,148 --> 00:13:11,690
are they acting like a filing cabinet

307
00:13:11,690 --> 00:13:14,090
or are they doing something more?

308
00:13:14,090 --> 00:13:17,290
It's a little bit hard to say, because if you train on enough

309
00:13:17,290 --> 00:13:19,930
data-- and these are trained on billions or trillions of data

310
00:13:19,930 --> 00:13:22,290
points-- if you trained on enough data and you just

311
00:13:22,290 --> 00:13:26,170
memorize the data, well, your test conditions might look

312
00:13:26,170 --> 00:13:27,930
identical to your--

313
00:13:27,930 --> 00:13:29,670
if I've trained on infinite things,

314
00:13:29,670 --> 00:13:32,930
then my test cases are just the same as my training data.

315
00:13:32,930 --> 00:13:36,380
So are they close to that-- just memorizing nearly infinite

316
00:13:36,380 --> 00:13:38,660
things, or are they not?

317
00:13:38,660 --> 00:13:41,580
So LLM-- if you don't know, this is Large Language Model--

318
00:13:41,580 --> 00:13:46,620
ChatGPT, latest greatest deep nets are in that family.

319
00:13:46,620 --> 00:13:50,800
So big model-- so is it acting like a filing cabinet?

320
00:13:50,800 --> 00:13:54,140
Can we actually measure if it's generalizing or not?

321
00:13:54,140 --> 00:13:56,620
So here's the question we can ask.

322
00:13:56,620 --> 00:13:58,380
How big would the filing cabinet have

323
00:13:58,380 --> 00:14:02,340
to be in order to do the feats of prediction

324
00:14:02,340 --> 00:14:07,300
that ChatGPT or some big LLM can do?

325
00:14:07,300 --> 00:14:09,420
So remember our model of the filing cabinet

326
00:14:09,420 --> 00:14:12,100
is just, we return 0 if we have a data point that we've never

327
00:14:12,100 --> 00:14:14,340
seen before.

328
00:14:14,340 --> 00:14:16,280
So here's just a simple counting experiment.

329
00:14:16,280 --> 00:14:18,655
I think you can make more sophisticated versions to this,

330
00:14:18,655 --> 00:14:20,960
but I found it fun.

331
00:14:20,960 --> 00:14:23,980
So we'll sample, a sequence--

332
00:14:23,980 --> 00:14:27,140
so language models are deep nets that take sequences of words

333
00:14:27,140 --> 00:14:29,580
as input and make some prediction from them.

334
00:14:29,580 --> 00:14:33,100
So we'll sample a sequence of n words,

335
00:14:33,100 --> 00:14:37,850
and we'll be sampling from a vocab of size m.

336
00:14:37,850 --> 00:14:39,170
So we have m nouns--

337
00:14:39,170 --> 00:14:42,150
1,000 different nouns, and we make a sentence of length--

338
00:14:42,150 --> 00:14:44,450
or a set of a sequence of nouns of length 5.

339
00:14:44,450 --> 00:14:46,190
So then n would be 5.

340
00:14:46,190 --> 00:14:50,790
So how many unique input sequences are there?

341
00:14:50,790 --> 00:14:55,190
So just simple combinatorics, m to the n.

342
00:14:55,190 --> 00:14:58,650
So I have m possible words, and I have n of them in a row.

343
00:14:58,650 --> 00:15:00,550
I sample independently each one.

344
00:15:00,550 --> 00:15:02,910
That's the number of such sequences.

345
00:15:02,910 --> 00:15:06,630
So now, we can ask, well, if we take a random such sequence

346
00:15:06,630 --> 00:15:08,910
and we input it into our model, did the model

347
00:15:08,910 --> 00:15:10,352
make a reasonable prediction?

348
00:15:10,352 --> 00:15:12,810
So technically, if this is our model of the filing cabinet,

349
00:15:12,810 --> 00:15:15,018
we're just asking, did it make a non-zero prediction?

350
00:15:15,018 --> 00:15:18,618
But did it output a correct prediction?

351
00:15:18,618 --> 00:15:20,410
So we can take our favorite language model.

352
00:15:20,410 --> 00:15:24,310
We can ask our favorite question about the input sequence

353
00:15:24,310 --> 00:15:26,690
and have the language model try to solve that question

354
00:15:26,690 --> 00:15:27,565
That's how they work.

355
00:15:27,565 --> 00:15:30,590
You can prompt them to answer different questions

356
00:15:30,590 --> 00:15:32,190
about your input sequence.

357
00:15:32,190 --> 00:15:34,770
We'll ask how often is the output--

358
00:15:34,770 --> 00:15:38,210
something that's non-trivial-- not what a filing cabinet would

359
00:15:38,210 --> 00:15:39,250
do.

360
00:15:39,250 --> 00:15:42,090
And then we can say that the filing cabinet, in order

361
00:15:42,090 --> 00:15:47,170
to get a score of s, equals the number of unique sequences

362
00:15:47,170 --> 00:15:49,250
times the percent of times those sequences mapped

363
00:15:49,250 --> 00:15:50,510
to something non-trivial.

364
00:15:50,510 --> 00:15:52,370
It would have to be that big.

365
00:15:52,370 --> 00:15:55,290
It'd have to be taking that many unique sequences

366
00:15:55,290 --> 00:15:58,810
and achieving this proportion of correct predictions

367
00:15:58,810 --> 00:16:01,050
would have to have that many cabinets.

368
00:16:01,050 --> 00:16:03,210
And if that number is really, really big--

369
00:16:03,210 --> 00:16:05,335
bigger than we think language models are, or bigger

370
00:16:05,335 --> 00:16:07,335
than the amount of data they've been trained on,

371
00:16:07,335 --> 00:16:09,050
then we know that language models are not

372
00:16:09,050 --> 00:16:11,250
doing pure memorization.

373
00:16:11,250 --> 00:16:15,830
So you can make up your own version of this,

374
00:16:15,830 --> 00:16:17,950
but just to convince myself, I ran it like this.

375
00:16:17,950 --> 00:16:20,130
I took 30 fruit names--

376
00:16:20,130 --> 00:16:23,430
apple, orange, pear, whatever.

377
00:16:23,430 --> 00:16:28,610
So my vocab is size 30, and my sequence length is size 10.

378
00:16:28,610 --> 00:16:32,180
So I'm going to sample 10 random fruits from that list.

379
00:16:32,180 --> 00:16:39,380
So there's 10 to the 30th possible input sequences.

380
00:16:39,380 --> 00:16:41,140
And then I'm going to have my language

381
00:16:41,140 --> 00:16:44,580
model answer this question about those sequences.

382
00:16:44,580 --> 00:16:46,620
So we'll talk more about language models

383
00:16:46,620 --> 00:16:47,560
and prompting later.

384
00:16:47,560 --> 00:16:50,460
But just you've all done this with things like ChatGPT

385
00:16:50,460 --> 00:16:52,040
or Claude and so forth.

386
00:16:52,040 --> 00:16:53,880
You can say how many citrus fruits--

387
00:16:53,880 --> 00:16:57,740
this is just my toy problem I'm giving it to try to estimate

388
00:16:57,740 --> 00:16:58,600
these probabilities.

389
00:16:58,600 --> 00:17:00,480
How many citrus fruits are in this list?

390
00:17:00,480 --> 00:17:02,020
And I give it this random sequence

391
00:17:02,020 --> 00:17:04,940
of 10 fruits sampled from that vocab of size 30.

392
00:17:04,940 --> 00:17:06,819
And it gets it correct.

393
00:17:06,819 --> 00:17:08,240
And so I did this 5 times.

394
00:17:08,240 --> 00:17:10,240
I'm not saying this is like the best experiment,

395
00:17:10,240 --> 00:17:13,400
but it got 5 out of 5, so it's correct every single time.

396
00:17:13,400 --> 00:17:15,619
So my estimate for the probability of being correct

397
00:17:15,619 --> 00:17:17,220
is 1.

398
00:17:17,220 --> 00:17:21,980
So now, we can just estimate-- you have p times m to the n

399
00:17:21,980 --> 00:17:24,700
is going to be 100 trillion.

400
00:17:24,700 --> 00:17:28,710
So the filing cabinet would have to have 100 trillion files.

401
00:17:28,710 --> 00:17:31,950
Also the training data-- the way the filing cabinet works

402
00:17:31,950 --> 00:17:35,292
is it only inputs a new data point into the cabinet

403
00:17:35,292 --> 00:17:37,250
if it sees that data point in the training set.

404
00:17:37,250 --> 00:17:38,917
So it means the training set has to have

405
00:17:38,917 --> 00:17:42,590
100 million examples for that model-- sorry, 100 trillion.

406
00:17:42,590 --> 00:17:44,310
And we think that ChatGPT and things

407
00:17:44,310 --> 00:17:47,490
like this are trained on more like tens of trillions.

408
00:17:47,490 --> 00:17:49,040
So I think I exceeded that.

409
00:17:49,040 --> 00:17:50,790
But you could always make this list longer

410
00:17:50,790 --> 00:17:52,930
and run the same experiment-- get it to a quadrillion,

411
00:17:52,930 --> 00:17:55,388
and really convince yourself that these things are not just

412
00:17:55,388 --> 00:17:57,510
memorizing.

413
00:17:57,510 --> 00:17:59,010
Another just for fun one.

414
00:17:59,010 --> 00:18:02,485
We could do the same thing, but we can use a text-to-image

415
00:18:02,485 --> 00:18:04,110
model, and now have it draw the fruits,

416
00:18:04,110 --> 00:18:06,910
and ask how many unique different--

417
00:18:06,910 --> 00:18:09,170
how often will it be correct in drawing the fruits?

418
00:18:09,170 --> 00:18:10,723
So I asked that to--

419
00:18:10,723 --> 00:18:12,390
ChatGPT can do both of these things now.

420
00:18:12,390 --> 00:18:14,075
These are kind of multimodal models.

421
00:18:14,075 --> 00:18:15,450
So you can say draw these fruits.

422
00:18:15,450 --> 00:18:17,470
I sample a random sequence of 10 fruits

423
00:18:17,470 --> 00:18:20,350
and I check if it's correct.

424
00:18:20,350 --> 00:18:21,870
Is this one correct?

425
00:18:21,870 --> 00:18:23,710
AUDIENCE: No.

426
00:18:23,710 --> 00:18:24,897
PHILLIP ISOLA: I said no.

427
00:18:24,897 --> 00:18:25,730
It's almost correct.

428
00:18:25,730 --> 00:18:26,780
I think it got 9 out of 10.

429
00:18:26,780 --> 00:18:27,980
I don't think it has a passion fruit.

430
00:18:27,980 --> 00:18:30,272
But anyway, I don't know what passion fruits look like.

431
00:18:30,272 --> 00:18:32,700
But I called this one incorrect.

432
00:18:32,700 --> 00:18:34,000
And so I estimated--

433
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:35,080
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

434
00:18:35,080 --> 00:18:36,160
PHILLIP ISOLA: Does have another wrong thing?

435
00:18:36,160 --> 00:18:36,580
OK.

436
00:18:36,580 --> 00:18:36,840
Yeah?

437
00:18:36,840 --> 00:18:37,560
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

438
00:18:37,560 --> 00:18:38,560
PHILLIP ISOLA: Cantaloupe or--

439
00:18:38,560 --> 00:18:39,185
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

440
00:18:39,185 --> 00:18:40,280
AUDIENCE: Coconut as well.

441
00:18:40,280 --> 00:18:41,580
PHILLIP ISOLA: Oh, yeah, coconuts missing.

442
00:18:41,580 --> 00:18:43,247
Maybe that was a passion fruit up there.

443
00:18:43,247 --> 00:18:45,200
But anyway, this one's wrong.

444
00:18:45,200 --> 00:18:48,280
But I ran this eight times, and one out of the eight times

445
00:18:48,280 --> 00:18:49,540
I thought it got it correct.

446
00:18:49,540 --> 00:18:52,040
I really saw each of the fruits represented.

447
00:18:52,040 --> 00:18:56,520
So now, our proportion p is 0.125, 1 over 8.

448
00:18:56,520 --> 00:18:58,480
And that means that you'd have to have a filing

449
00:18:58,480 --> 00:19:00,560
cabinet of size 12 trillion.

450
00:19:00,560 --> 00:19:04,280
So I don't think this has been 12 trillion images,

451
00:19:04,280 --> 00:19:05,520
but it's hard to know.

452
00:19:05,520 --> 00:19:07,430
OpenAI is secretive.

453
00:19:07,430 --> 00:19:09,180
But you can do these types of experiments,

454
00:19:09,180 --> 00:19:11,760
and I think you can pretty quickly convince yourself

455
00:19:11,760 --> 00:19:14,440
that the way that modern deep nets are working

456
00:19:14,440 --> 00:19:16,100
is not like a filing cabinet.

457
00:19:16,100 --> 00:19:17,900
It has some properties in common with the filing cabinet.

458
00:19:17,900 --> 00:19:19,720
We'll come back to that, but that doesn't fully

459
00:19:19,720 --> 00:19:20,845
explain what they're doing.

460
00:19:26,077 --> 00:19:28,160
Yeah, and so just a suggestion for final project--

461
00:19:28,160 --> 00:19:29,400
I think it'd be fun to really do that

462
00:19:29,400 --> 00:19:31,260
systematically and rigorously, and try

463
00:19:31,260 --> 00:19:34,060
to actually estimate how many unique concepts these things

464
00:19:34,060 --> 00:19:34,860
have.

465
00:19:34,860 --> 00:19:36,540
I haven't really seen very many papers

466
00:19:36,540 --> 00:19:41,620
that do this type of experiment, but I think it'd be fun.

467
00:19:41,620 --> 00:19:44,300
So I got kind of excited about the feats

468
00:19:44,300 --> 00:19:48,260
of generalization of deep nets in this project

469
00:19:48,260 --> 00:19:50,220
from a few years ago.

470
00:19:50,220 --> 00:19:53,780
And so what we did here is we trained a deep net

471
00:19:53,780 --> 00:19:58,540
to take as input sketches and produces output photos of cats

472
00:19:58,540 --> 00:20:00,340
in this case.

473
00:20:00,340 --> 00:20:02,980
So a neural net just maps.

474
00:20:02,980 --> 00:20:05,540
And we'll talk about these generative models

475
00:20:05,540 --> 00:20:08,200
and image-to-image models a little bit later in the course.

476
00:20:08,200 --> 00:20:10,500
But for now, it's just a mapping from x

477
00:20:10,500 --> 00:20:14,740
to y-- it happens to be sketches to photos.

478
00:20:14,740 --> 00:20:16,740
And so what we did in this project

479
00:20:16,740 --> 00:20:18,980
is we didn't use human hand-drawn sketches

480
00:20:18,980 --> 00:20:20,980
because those were expensive to acquire.

481
00:20:20,980 --> 00:20:23,770
So instead, we just use this thing called HED,

482
00:20:23,770 --> 00:20:25,170
which is like an edge detector.

483
00:20:25,170 --> 00:20:27,503
And it's like a proxy for what a human might have drawn,

484
00:20:27,503 --> 00:20:30,390
but it doesn't quite match what a human would really draw.

485
00:20:30,390 --> 00:20:33,690
So from a kind of naive machine learning perspective,

486
00:20:33,690 --> 00:20:38,670
you would say, if I train this on a ton of examples

487
00:20:38,670 --> 00:20:41,970
of edge maps and cats, and I give it a new edge map,

488
00:20:41,970 --> 00:20:44,830
it should predict a new cat because this is just

489
00:20:44,830 --> 00:20:47,710
generalization to independently and identically sampled

490
00:20:47,710 --> 00:20:50,330
data points from the same population.

491
00:20:50,330 --> 00:20:53,510
So that's like classical generalization theory

492
00:20:53,510 --> 00:20:55,750
in machine learning.

493
00:20:55,750 --> 00:20:57,922
But the really interesting thing is

494
00:20:57,922 --> 00:21:00,130
it also worked when you give it hand-drawn cats which

495
00:21:00,130 --> 00:21:01,690
it had never been trained on.

496
00:21:01,690 --> 00:21:04,430
So these are inputs that are actually out of distribution--

497
00:21:04,430 --> 00:21:07,310
systematically different than what it was trained on.

498
00:21:07,310 --> 00:21:12,290
So in the prehistory of deep learning,

499
00:21:12,290 --> 00:21:15,150
this was the popular thing of the day.

500
00:21:15,150 --> 00:21:17,570
Now, of course we've seen much more exciting things.

501
00:21:17,570 --> 00:21:19,790
But if I sketch a cat, it doesn't look anything

502
00:21:19,790 --> 00:21:20,610
like an edge map.

503
00:21:20,610 --> 00:21:25,040
And it just works, and it's still cool even today.

504
00:21:25,040 --> 00:21:27,820
So people had a lot of fun.

505
00:21:27,820 --> 00:21:30,520
[LAUGHTER]

506
00:21:30,520 --> 00:21:32,980
And this is generalization.

507
00:21:32,980 --> 00:21:35,060
It's never been trained on these types of things.

508
00:21:35,060 --> 00:21:37,640
It has some kind of creativity almost.

509
00:21:37,640 --> 00:21:40,060
We have to be careful not to anthropomorphize too much,

510
00:21:40,060 --> 00:21:43,520
but there's something here which is a little bit magical

511
00:21:43,520 --> 00:21:45,800
and not fully understood.

512
00:21:45,800 --> 00:21:48,080
But I think we can start to get a sense of what's

513
00:21:48,080 --> 00:21:50,628
going on with an experiment like this.

514
00:21:50,628 --> 00:21:52,420
So I'm just trying to build up intuitions--

515
00:21:52,420 --> 00:21:54,340
do simple kind of experiments.

516
00:21:54,340 --> 00:21:57,260
We'll get into some more technical theory in a bit.

517
00:21:57,260 --> 00:21:58,560
But here we go.

518
00:21:58,560 --> 00:22:02,048
I just draw two eyes.

519
00:22:02,048 --> 00:22:03,340
Now, what if I draw three eyes?

520
00:22:03,340 --> 00:22:05,418
What's going to happen?

521
00:22:05,418 --> 00:22:07,460
It's never seen a photo of a cat with three eyes.

522
00:22:07,460 --> 00:22:08,720
How would it know what to do?

523
00:22:08,720 --> 00:22:09,780
But it works.

524
00:22:09,780 --> 00:22:13,700
It just puts that eye where that eye was drawn.

525
00:22:13,700 --> 00:22:14,500
A lot of eyes.

526
00:22:18,305 --> 00:22:19,520
You can add the body.

527
00:22:19,520 --> 00:22:20,940
That makes sense.

528
00:22:20,940 --> 00:22:23,800
So how is it doing this?

529
00:22:23,800 --> 00:22:25,260
How is it generalizing to more eyes

530
00:22:25,260 --> 00:22:27,460
than it ever saw at training time?

531
00:22:27,460 --> 00:22:31,800
And I think there's not a single explanation,

532
00:22:31,800 --> 00:22:33,620
but the best one I have in mind is

533
00:22:33,620 --> 00:22:36,980
it's the inductive biases of convolutional nets.

534
00:22:36,980 --> 00:22:38,653
So this network was a ConvNet.

535
00:22:38,653 --> 00:22:39,320
We've seen that.

536
00:22:39,320 --> 00:22:41,220
What do ConvNets do?

537
00:22:41,220 --> 00:22:47,420
They take an input, they chop it up into patches,

538
00:22:47,420 --> 00:22:51,220
and then they independently and identically process every patch.

539
00:22:51,220 --> 00:22:54,260
So they're factorizing the problem

540
00:22:54,260 --> 00:22:57,020
into a lot of independent decisions.

541
00:22:57,020 --> 00:23:00,180
And when you decompose something into these independent

542
00:23:00,180 --> 00:23:02,660
components, you get what's called compositionality,

543
00:23:02,660 --> 00:23:08,260
where you can take a new composition of things

544
00:23:08,260 --> 00:23:10,440
and factor them into these independent components,

545
00:23:10,440 --> 00:23:11,420
these patches.

546
00:23:11,420 --> 00:23:14,460
And it should it should know what to do,

547
00:23:14,460 --> 00:23:18,670
because it only ever has to imitate the training

548
00:23:18,670 --> 00:23:19,612
data on these patches.

549
00:23:19,612 --> 00:23:21,070
And then the combination of patches

550
00:23:21,070 --> 00:23:24,190
is just the rule of stitch them together.

551
00:23:24,190 --> 00:23:27,070
So for every single patch, it's doing--

552
00:23:27,070 --> 00:23:28,830
in distribution generalization, it's

553
00:23:28,830 --> 00:23:31,965
seen a lot of things that look like ovals, and they match eyes.

554
00:23:31,965 --> 00:23:33,590
And then it can generalize to more eyes

555
00:23:33,590 --> 00:23:40,940
because I've just factorized the problem in this simple way.

556
00:23:40,940 --> 00:23:46,110
So architectural constraints and biases

557
00:23:46,110 --> 00:23:48,590
and the way that we enforce symmetries and factorization

558
00:23:48,590 --> 00:23:51,190
into our models allows you to do certain kinds

559
00:23:51,190 --> 00:23:52,710
of feats of generalization.

560
00:23:52,710 --> 00:23:57,230
And we saw that with the ability of graph nets

561
00:23:57,230 --> 00:24:00,190
to generalize to new permutations of their inputs,

562
00:24:00,190 --> 00:24:01,870
because they're permutation-invariant or

563
00:24:01,870 --> 00:24:02,650
equivariant.

564
00:24:02,650 --> 00:24:04,697
So that's just built into the architecture.

565
00:24:04,697 --> 00:24:06,530
I don't have to have seen every permutation.

566
00:24:06,530 --> 00:24:08,010
I can just have seen some permutations,

567
00:24:08,010 --> 00:24:09,630
and then it will generalize to other permutations.

568
00:24:09,630 --> 00:24:11,185
That's baked into the architecture.

569
00:24:11,185 --> 00:24:13,310
It's not something you have to learn from the data.

570
00:24:13,310 --> 00:24:15,440
So architecture is one of the main levers

571
00:24:15,440 --> 00:24:19,080
we have for these feats of generalization that

572
00:24:19,080 --> 00:24:22,800
violate just statistical learning theory,

573
00:24:22,800 --> 00:24:25,660
or that go beyond just naive statistical learning theory.

574
00:24:30,320 --> 00:24:33,760
So let's get a little bit into generalization theory

575
00:24:33,760 --> 00:24:37,280
and start with the classic picture.

576
00:24:37,280 --> 00:24:42,800
So most of generalization theory comes from Occam.

577
00:24:42,800 --> 00:24:44,580
It comes from the Occam's razor idea.

578
00:24:44,580 --> 00:24:47,020
It's all variations on Occam's razor in some sense.

579
00:24:47,020 --> 00:24:48,760
So just to remind you, Occam's razor

580
00:24:48,760 --> 00:24:51,560
says among competing explanations that

581
00:24:51,560 --> 00:24:55,730
fit the data equally well, you should prefer the simplest

582
00:24:55,730 --> 00:24:57,480
And the generalization way of stating that

583
00:24:57,480 --> 00:24:59,720
is that the simplest model that fits the data

584
00:24:59,720 --> 00:25:01,725
will be most likely to be the true model.

585
00:25:01,725 --> 00:25:04,100
Therefore, it will be most likely to generalize the best.

586
00:25:07,080 --> 00:25:10,680
Just one little note is that I think sometimes people forget

587
00:25:10,680 --> 00:25:13,940
to add the "fits the data."

588
00:25:13,940 --> 00:25:16,855
So it's not true the simplest model is most likely to be true,

589
00:25:16,855 --> 00:25:18,480
or it's most likely to generalize best.

590
00:25:18,480 --> 00:25:20,438
It's the simplest model that fits the data that

591
00:25:20,438 --> 00:25:21,900
actually explains the data.

592
00:25:21,900 --> 00:25:25,860
You need that, otherwise you get these absurd statements like,

593
00:25:25,860 --> 00:25:27,920
oh, well, I should use a linear model.

594
00:25:27,920 --> 00:25:29,280
But no, it doesn't fit the data.

595
00:25:29,280 --> 00:25:32,247
So you have to use a complex enough model that fits the data,

596
00:25:32,247 --> 00:25:34,080
but then you want to prefer the simpler one.

597
00:25:34,080 --> 00:25:35,740
And there's a lot of deep arguments for why

598
00:25:35,740 --> 00:25:36,740
that should be the case.

599
00:25:36,740 --> 00:25:39,340
It's intuitively clear from the idea of Occam's razor

600
00:25:39,340 --> 00:25:43,420
that we learn in grade school or whenever we learn it.

601
00:25:43,420 --> 00:25:44,940
But also there's deep mathematics

602
00:25:44,940 --> 00:25:46,900
that can be used to derive formal versions

603
00:25:46,900 --> 00:25:48,020
of this statement.

604
00:25:48,020 --> 00:25:50,260
Yeah, question?

605
00:25:50,260 --> 00:25:56,940
AUDIENCE: If someone were to compare this [INAUDIBLE]

606
00:25:56,940 --> 00:25:59,940
that all models [INAUDIBLE].

607
00:25:59,940 --> 00:26:01,967
How would you compare this [INAUDIBLE]?

608
00:26:01,967 --> 00:26:04,300
PHILLIP ISOLA: The question-- that's really interesting.

609
00:26:04,300 --> 00:26:05,920
There's another quote-- "all models are wrong,

610
00:26:05,920 --> 00:26:07,000
but some are useful."

611
00:26:11,020 --> 00:26:15,910
Yeah, that's intriguing to think about.

612
00:26:15,910 --> 00:26:21,870
I would say that the "all models" is--

613
00:26:21,870 --> 00:26:24,110
so this is only the set of models

614
00:26:24,110 --> 00:26:27,470
that are in the equivalence class of all equally well

615
00:26:27,470 --> 00:26:28,950
fitting the data.

616
00:26:28,950 --> 00:26:31,190
So we're not really considering models

617
00:26:31,190 --> 00:26:35,070
that are wrong in terms of only approximating the data.

618
00:26:35,070 --> 00:26:36,630
So it's a slightly different setting

619
00:26:36,630 --> 00:26:40,990
that we're looking at there.

620
00:26:40,990 --> 00:26:42,310
But yeah, I don't know.

621
00:26:42,310 --> 00:26:44,250
There's probably more discussion around that,

622
00:26:44,250 --> 00:26:46,170
but I don't have a crisp answer right now.

623
00:26:46,170 --> 00:26:47,390
Yeah, question?

624
00:26:47,390 --> 00:26:50,350
AUDIENCE: Has this statement been proven rigorously or is it

625
00:26:50,350 --> 00:26:51,437
a heuristic?

626
00:26:51,437 --> 00:26:53,270
PHILLIP ISOLA: So I would say in this form--

627
00:26:53,270 --> 00:26:54,770
so the question-- has this statement

628
00:26:54,770 --> 00:26:57,190
been proven rigorously, or is it a heuristic?

629
00:26:57,190 --> 00:27:00,230
In this form, it's a heuristic because how do we

630
00:27:00,230 --> 00:27:01,970
measure simplest?

631
00:27:01,970 --> 00:27:03,650
How do we measure generalization?

632
00:27:03,650 --> 00:27:05,150
We need to make these mathematically

633
00:27:05,150 --> 00:27:06,690
precise to make it rigorous.

634
00:27:06,690 --> 00:27:12,040
But there are rigorous versions of the Occam's razor argument.

635
00:27:12,040 --> 00:27:15,420
My favorite one is this one from various people,

636
00:27:15,420 --> 00:27:23,320
including Solomonoff, which is that the shortest program that

637
00:27:23,320 --> 00:27:25,240
perfectly fits or generates the data--

638
00:27:25,240 --> 00:27:27,960
that just outputs the data set--

639
00:27:27,960 --> 00:27:30,720
is the one that will generalize best and make the best

640
00:27:30,720 --> 00:27:31,510
predictions.

641
00:27:31,510 --> 00:27:33,760
There's an equivalent version of this statement, which

642
00:27:33,760 --> 00:27:37,240
is that the most compressed representation of your data

643
00:27:37,240 --> 00:27:38,797
is the best model of your data.

644
00:27:38,797 --> 00:27:40,880
There's some equivalency between these two things.

645
00:27:40,880 --> 00:27:44,080
So I'm not going to get into all the technical details of that.

646
00:27:44,080 --> 00:27:47,220
But this is the place to start reading

647
00:27:47,220 --> 00:27:50,840
if you want mathematically precise arguments for Occam's

648
00:27:50,840 --> 00:27:52,400
razor.

649
00:27:52,400 --> 00:27:55,600
And the problem, though, is that finding the shortest

650
00:27:55,600 --> 00:27:57,697
program that fits the data requires

651
00:27:57,697 --> 00:27:59,780
searching over the space of all possible programs.

652
00:27:59,780 --> 00:28:03,920
Imagine searching over the space of all possible Python programs.

653
00:28:03,920 --> 00:28:05,322
You just can't do that.

654
00:28:05,322 --> 00:28:07,280
And deep learning doesn't search over the space

655
00:28:07,280 --> 00:28:08,390
of all possible programs.

656
00:28:08,390 --> 00:28:10,765
It just searches over the space of all weights and biases

657
00:28:10,765 --> 00:28:12,890
of a particular model architecture.

658
00:28:12,890 --> 00:28:15,090
So this is completely intractable,

659
00:28:15,090 --> 00:28:17,570
but it comes up in algorithmic information theory

660
00:28:17,570 --> 00:28:19,130
and areas like that.

661
00:28:19,130 --> 00:28:21,010
So it's good to keep in mind, this

662
00:28:21,010 --> 00:28:23,970
is kind of like the mathematical basis for generalization theory.

663
00:28:23,970 --> 00:28:28,370
But actually finding the optimal program or the shortest program

664
00:28:28,370 --> 00:28:30,910
is intractable, so we're not going to quite do that.

665
00:28:30,910 --> 00:28:33,790
But this is what we want.

666
00:28:33,790 --> 00:28:36,610
We want a way of measuring simplicity or complexity

667
00:28:36,610 --> 00:28:43,810
of a hypothesis space or of a solution to a problem.

668
00:28:43,810 --> 00:28:47,547
And this is kind of the best notion of simplicity,

669
00:28:47,547 --> 00:28:48,630
but we can't quite use it.

670
00:28:48,630 --> 00:28:51,130
So what else can we use?

671
00:28:51,130 --> 00:28:55,370
So let's do a quick review of overfitting--

672
00:28:55,370 --> 00:28:58,410
the idea of overfitting and the bias variance trade-off.

673
00:28:58,410 --> 00:29:00,947
So remember that the test error is

674
00:29:00,947 --> 00:29:03,530
equal to the train error plus the difference between the train

675
00:29:03,530 --> 00:29:06,870
error and the test error.

676
00:29:06,870 --> 00:29:09,162
And we call the training error the bias

677
00:29:09,162 --> 00:29:10,870
and the difference between the test error

678
00:29:10,870 --> 00:29:14,830
and the train error the variance.

679
00:29:14,830 --> 00:29:17,350
And the kind of classical machine learning 101--

680
00:29:17,350 --> 00:29:18,750
picture of how things work--

681
00:29:18,750 --> 00:29:23,130
is that as I increase the kind of capacity,

682
00:29:23,130 --> 00:29:24,950
the number of functions under consideration

683
00:29:24,950 --> 00:29:26,967
in my hypothesis space that I'm searching over,

684
00:29:26,967 --> 00:29:28,550
as I make bigger and bigger and bigger

685
00:29:28,550 --> 00:29:33,350
deep nets with more and more and more parameters, then eventually

686
00:29:33,350 --> 00:29:34,950
I'll get universal approximation.

687
00:29:34,950 --> 00:29:37,530
If I get width goes to infinity, I can fit any function.

688
00:29:37,530 --> 00:29:40,730
So I can minimize my loss on the training error,

689
00:29:40,730 --> 00:29:42,870
so the training risk goes down.

690
00:29:42,870 --> 00:29:46,230
But I'll start overfitting, and the test risk will eventually

691
00:29:46,230 --> 00:29:49,670
go up, because I'm finding fits to the data which

692
00:29:49,670 --> 00:29:52,750
are kind of fitting to properties of the data that

693
00:29:52,750 --> 00:29:54,410
don't actually generalize, like noise,

694
00:29:54,410 --> 00:29:57,710
or the fact that I got this particular batch of samples

695
00:29:57,710 --> 00:30:00,070
as opposed to a different batch of samples.

696
00:30:00,070 --> 00:30:05,800
So this is the overfitting scenario that you have likely

697
00:30:05,800 --> 00:30:07,880
seen before.

698
00:30:07,880 --> 00:30:10,680
And one of the ways of measuring model capacity-- modeling

699
00:30:10,680 --> 00:30:15,320
the x-axis or measuring the complexity of a hypothesis space

700
00:30:15,320 --> 00:30:16,660
is via number of parameters.

701
00:30:16,660 --> 00:30:17,760
And this is one of the classical ways

702
00:30:17,760 --> 00:30:19,093
that you might have encountered.

703
00:30:19,093 --> 00:30:21,748
If I want to find my best machine learning model,

704
00:30:21,748 --> 00:30:23,540
I need to control the number of parameters.

705
00:30:23,540 --> 00:30:26,040
If I have too many parameters, it will overfit.

706
00:30:26,040 --> 00:30:28,440
So let's look at what happens if I'm

707
00:30:28,440 --> 00:30:33,520
doing some kind of polynomial regression onto these red points

708
00:30:33,520 --> 00:30:34,160
here.

709
00:30:34,160 --> 00:30:39,280
So the orange line is my fit to the data.

710
00:30:39,280 --> 00:30:41,720
And if I use a degree 1 polynomial--

711
00:30:41,720 --> 00:30:43,220
I think it's not quite polynomials.

712
00:30:43,220 --> 00:30:45,400
It's a particular family of polynomials.

713
00:30:45,400 --> 00:30:49,640
I'm finding the coefficients of a weighted sum

714
00:30:49,640 --> 00:30:52,240
of a particular family of simple functions,

715
00:30:52,240 --> 00:30:54,620
like Legendre polynomials, I think, in this case.

716
00:30:54,620 --> 00:30:56,760
But it doesn't matter too much.

717
00:30:56,760 --> 00:30:59,080
So I'm going to say with d equals

718
00:30:59,080 --> 00:31:01,660
1 is the degree of that family of polynomials.

719
00:31:01,660 --> 00:31:04,330
It's just d equals 1 means linear,

720
00:31:04,330 --> 00:31:06,490
and a linear fit can't do a great job.

721
00:31:06,490 --> 00:31:08,290
The truth function is the blue line,

722
00:31:08,290 --> 00:31:12,490
and the training data is the red points.

723
00:31:12,490 --> 00:31:16,930
What happens as I increase the wiggliness of the polynomials

724
00:31:16,930 --> 00:31:17,710
in my family?

725
00:31:17,710 --> 00:31:19,610
I include more and more polynomials

726
00:31:19,610 --> 00:31:21,190
that are higher and higher frequency

727
00:31:21,190 --> 00:31:23,850
and the kind of wiggles that they can express.

728
00:31:23,850 --> 00:31:27,950
So if I go up to a quadratic or cubic model in this case-- well,

729
00:31:27,950 --> 00:31:30,250
the ground truth function happened to be cubic

730
00:31:30,250 --> 00:31:33,770
or happened to have order 3 Legendre polynomial,

731
00:31:33,770 --> 00:31:36,150
and I get a really good fit.

732
00:31:36,150 --> 00:31:40,530
And then overfitting is, if I go up to--

733
00:31:40,530 --> 00:31:42,350
have too many degrees of freedom,

734
00:31:42,350 --> 00:31:46,910
I can actually not only recover the true data,

735
00:31:46,910 --> 00:31:48,510
but I'm also fitting to the noise.

736
00:31:48,510 --> 00:31:51,690
This deviation from the blue line is just noise.

737
00:31:51,690 --> 00:31:56,010
And the model is wildly deviating

738
00:31:56,010 --> 00:31:59,450
in order to capture that aspect that doesn't actually

739
00:31:59,450 --> 00:31:59,950
generalize.

740
00:31:59,950 --> 00:32:01,575
It doesn't actually tell them something

741
00:32:01,575 --> 00:32:03,930
about the true underlying function.

742
00:32:03,930 --> 00:32:06,050
So this is our classic picture of overfitting,

743
00:32:06,050 --> 00:32:09,910
but here's the surprise if you haven't seen it before.

744
00:32:09,910 --> 00:32:14,590
What happens if I go not to d equals 20, but d equals 1,000?

745
00:32:14,590 --> 00:32:17,000
I have a really, really expressive class

746
00:32:17,000 --> 00:32:18,750
of polynomials that can fit really, really

747
00:32:18,750 --> 00:32:20,470
crazy wiggly functions.

748
00:32:20,470 --> 00:32:23,790
Is it going to go more crazy and overfit even worse in the sense

749
00:32:23,790 --> 00:32:27,990
that these deviations will just go to infinity,

750
00:32:27,990 --> 00:32:29,630
or will it do something different?

751
00:32:29,630 --> 00:32:31,378
What do you think?

752
00:32:31,378 --> 00:32:33,170
Some of you probably have seen this before,

753
00:32:33,170 --> 00:32:34,050
but let's go up here.

754
00:32:34,050 --> 00:32:34,550
Yeah?

755
00:32:34,550 --> 00:32:37,470
AUDIENCE: I mean, you should have a large manifold of fits

756
00:32:37,470 --> 00:32:38,918
that all fit perfectly, right?

757
00:32:38,918 --> 00:32:39,710
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yes.

758
00:32:39,710 --> 00:32:41,590
AUDIENCE: So some of them could be good, some of them

759
00:32:41,590 --> 00:32:42,210
could be bad.

760
00:32:42,210 --> 00:32:43,690
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, so the answer to--

761
00:32:43,690 --> 00:32:45,398
and that's a quite sophisticated answer--

762
00:32:45,398 --> 00:32:48,230
is you should have a large set of functions

763
00:32:48,230 --> 00:32:50,350
that all perfectly fit the data.

764
00:32:50,350 --> 00:32:53,110
And so some of them should be good and some of them

765
00:32:53,110 --> 00:32:53,715
should be bad.

766
00:32:53,715 --> 00:32:55,090
But which one will you arrive at?

767
00:32:55,090 --> 00:32:56,923
Well, it's going to depend on other factors.

768
00:32:56,923 --> 00:32:58,430
It's going to depend on how you're

769
00:32:58,430 --> 00:33:01,020
searching over that space, or what optimizer are you using?

770
00:33:01,020 --> 00:33:03,298
How are you minimizing your training error?

771
00:33:03,298 --> 00:33:04,840
It's going to depend on other factors

772
00:33:04,840 --> 00:33:11,480
beyond just randomly selecting one of the solutions that

773
00:33:11,480 --> 00:33:14,140
fits the data.

774
00:33:14,140 --> 00:33:16,680
So here's what happens for this regression

775
00:33:16,680 --> 00:33:19,040
problem with these polynomials.

776
00:33:19,040 --> 00:33:23,880
So when d equals 1,000, you actually are able to fit this

777
00:33:23,880 --> 00:33:27,560
really spiky function, but it adheres much more closely

778
00:33:27,560 --> 00:33:31,760
to the true smooth solution.

779
00:33:31,760 --> 00:33:33,960
And this phenomena is called least--

780
00:33:33,960 --> 00:33:35,400
is called double descent.

781
00:33:35,400 --> 00:33:38,220
So it's the idea that overfitting is actually--

782
00:33:38,220 --> 00:33:39,720
the classical picture of overfitting

783
00:33:39,720 --> 00:33:41,560
is a little bit inaccurate.

784
00:33:41,560 --> 00:33:45,680
And what actually happens is you start overfitting really badly

785
00:33:45,680 --> 00:33:47,445
as you add more parameters.

786
00:33:47,445 --> 00:33:49,320
But eventually, if you add enough parameters,

787
00:33:49,320 --> 00:33:52,440
for certain types of models, including deep nets

788
00:33:52,440 --> 00:33:55,680
and also including these polynomial regression problems,

789
00:33:55,680 --> 00:33:59,130
you end up getting this kind of behavior

790
00:33:59,130 --> 00:34:01,490
where you start actually getting better generalization

791
00:34:01,490 --> 00:34:04,250
error with more parameters.

792
00:34:04,250 --> 00:34:07,410
And the basic hypothesis here is that--

793
00:34:07,410 --> 00:34:09,630
and this is provable for certain simple systems,

794
00:34:09,630 --> 00:34:12,010
but it's not really provable for deep nets.

795
00:34:12,010 --> 00:34:13,764
But the basic hypothesis for deep nets

796
00:34:13,764 --> 00:34:15,889
and the general statement is that the learned model

797
00:34:15,889 --> 00:34:18,730
has two components.

798
00:34:18,730 --> 00:34:22,889
With enough capacity, it will find a simple fit to the data.

799
00:34:22,889 --> 00:34:26,889
And then it will also use little spikes of capacity

800
00:34:26,889 --> 00:34:29,770
to fit the outliers.

801
00:34:29,770 --> 00:34:32,370
So there's some kind of simple component, which

802
00:34:32,370 --> 00:34:34,929
is predictive and generalizes well to new samples

803
00:34:34,929 --> 00:34:37,449
from the population, and a spiking component

804
00:34:37,449 --> 00:34:40,489
that overfits noise and things that don't generalize

805
00:34:40,489 --> 00:34:41,429
in the training data.

806
00:34:41,429 --> 00:34:42,590
So it's like memorization.

807
00:34:42,590 --> 00:34:44,449
Spikiness is like memorization.

808
00:34:44,449 --> 00:34:47,820
I'm just memorizing those--

809
00:34:47,820 --> 00:34:50,270
when my input is this, I memorized what it was.

810
00:34:50,270 --> 00:34:52,310
So it's memorization plus generalization.

811
00:34:52,310 --> 00:34:54,739
It's a really interesting combination

812
00:34:54,739 --> 00:35:00,040
that, until a few years ago, people didn't widely appreciate.

813
00:35:00,040 --> 00:35:02,460
There's a nice visualization of this with an MLP

814
00:35:02,460 --> 00:35:04,202
that you can find at this link.

815
00:35:04,202 --> 00:35:05,660
It looks basically the same as what

816
00:35:05,660 --> 00:35:08,920
I'm showing you, but not quite as spiky.

817
00:35:08,920 --> 00:35:12,980
So deep nets always are a little bit Messier

818
00:35:12,980 --> 00:35:15,700
than more classical models, so it's easier

819
00:35:15,700 --> 00:35:18,260
to see with this polynomial regression model.

820
00:35:18,260 --> 00:35:19,200
Yeah, question?

821
00:35:19,200 --> 00:35:20,860
AUDIENCE: So from my understanding,

822
00:35:20,860 --> 00:35:24,160
a polynomial regression is a closed form solution.

823
00:35:24,160 --> 00:35:26,220
So I guess you could see that this is always

824
00:35:26,220 --> 00:35:27,518
true in this case.

825
00:35:27,518 --> 00:35:29,060
But for a neural network where you're

826
00:35:29,060 --> 00:35:31,820
doing like a stochastic optimization process,

827
00:35:31,820 --> 00:35:34,180
I'm wondering how you can be sure you'll always

828
00:35:34,180 --> 00:35:38,940
arrive at this function and not, as you said, a function that

829
00:35:38,940 --> 00:35:40,113
is wildly inaccurate?

830
00:35:40,113 --> 00:35:41,780
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, so the question is,

831
00:35:41,780 --> 00:35:44,080
for polynomial regression, there's a closed form solution,

832
00:35:44,080 --> 00:35:46,413
and you can really prove that this will be what happens.

833
00:35:46,413 --> 00:35:49,700
And that solution will have this property if that's the case.

834
00:35:49,700 --> 00:35:52,860
For a neural net, the solution you arrive at

835
00:35:52,860 --> 00:35:54,663
will depend on the initialization,

836
00:35:54,663 --> 00:35:55,580
which might be random.

837
00:35:55,580 --> 00:35:57,520
It will depend on the stochastic mini batches,

838
00:35:57,520 --> 00:35:59,040
which are going to be random.

839
00:35:59,040 --> 00:36:01,360
There's a lot of randomness, and it's much harder

840
00:36:01,360 --> 00:36:04,560
to make clean statements about what solution you'll arrive at.

841
00:36:04,560 --> 00:36:06,820
So instead, we can make empirical statements.

842
00:36:06,820 --> 00:36:09,600
We can say that if we run this neural network over and over

843
00:36:09,600 --> 00:36:12,660
again with the Adam optimizer, or the SGD optimizer,

844
00:36:12,660 --> 00:36:13,920
here's what we observe.

845
00:36:13,920 --> 00:36:15,738
And I'll show you that on the next slide.

846
00:36:15,738 --> 00:36:17,280
Or we can make theoretical statements

847
00:36:17,280 --> 00:36:20,240
like, the distribution of solutions

848
00:36:20,240 --> 00:36:22,240
that will be arrived at under certain conditions

849
00:36:22,240 --> 00:36:26,240
will be smooth and spiky with high probability, stuff

850
00:36:26,240 --> 00:36:26,740
like this.

851
00:36:26,740 --> 00:36:28,400
Yep, one more question?

852
00:36:28,400 --> 00:36:30,400
AUDIENCE: When would you know that you're

853
00:36:30,400 --> 00:36:34,280
using the right computational resources rather

854
00:36:34,280 --> 00:36:37,100
than optimizing to a new model?

855
00:36:37,100 --> 00:36:42,260
So if it takes 1,000 to make an optimal solution,

856
00:36:42,260 --> 00:36:45,440
but it takes a lot of computational effort,

857
00:36:45,440 --> 00:36:50,120
should you stop at some point, or turn to other model that will

858
00:36:50,120 --> 00:36:52,130
potentially fit better?

859
00:36:52,130 --> 00:36:54,970
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, so when should you

860
00:36:54,970 --> 00:36:59,847
decide where to stop in an optimization process

861
00:36:59,847 --> 00:37:01,430
is the question, or at least stopping.

862
00:37:01,430 --> 00:37:04,130
Yeah, so that's a hard question.

863
00:37:04,130 --> 00:37:07,650
And so the number of steps of gradient descent that you do

864
00:37:07,650 --> 00:37:10,790
is kind of a hyperparameter, and it can affect the solution.

865
00:37:10,790 --> 00:37:13,230
And if you do too many, then you might be overfitting.

866
00:37:13,230 --> 00:37:14,850
And that's kind of a classical idea.

867
00:37:14,850 --> 00:37:18,510
But what we'll see is that in this neural net era,

868
00:37:18,510 --> 00:37:20,190
it tends to be just train forever.

869
00:37:20,190 --> 00:37:21,690
And the longer you train, the better

870
00:37:21,690 --> 00:37:24,530
you do because there's a double descent phenomenon, which

871
00:37:24,530 --> 00:37:27,410
I'll show in the next slide in terms of computation.

872
00:37:27,410 --> 00:37:29,130
The longer you train, the better you'll

873
00:37:29,130 --> 00:37:33,190
do in this particular family of models is the general principle.

874
00:37:33,190 --> 00:37:34,810
But yeah, of course, there's more

875
00:37:34,810 --> 00:37:38,083
that can be said about that.

876
00:37:38,083 --> 00:37:40,250
So this was a really cool paper from a few years ago

877
00:37:40,250 --> 00:37:43,240
that got people excited.

878
00:37:43,240 --> 00:37:45,490
So the double descent phenomenon is just a consequence

879
00:37:45,490 --> 00:37:46,910
of what I already showed you.

880
00:37:46,910 --> 00:37:50,100
So we have regular classical overfitting.

881
00:37:50,100 --> 00:37:53,405
As I increase my capacity of my hypothesis space, which we're

882
00:37:53,405 --> 00:37:55,780
right now naively calculating as the number of parameters

883
00:37:55,780 --> 00:37:59,360
in the hypothesis space, then my training error goes down,

884
00:37:59,360 --> 00:38:01,080
but my test error eventually goes up.

885
00:38:01,080 --> 00:38:02,260
I overfit.

886
00:38:02,260 --> 00:38:05,180
But then after overfitting, eventually,

887
00:38:05,180 --> 00:38:08,220
I learned the simple function and the spikes,

888
00:38:08,220 --> 00:38:13,860
and so I actually start to generalize better again.

889
00:38:13,860 --> 00:38:17,060
And there's this thing called interpolation threshold, which

890
00:38:17,060 --> 00:38:18,820
is the point at which I can perfectly

891
00:38:18,820 --> 00:38:20,820
memorize all the training data.

892
00:38:20,820 --> 00:38:22,175
And I think one intuitive--

893
00:38:22,175 --> 00:38:24,800
I'm not sure if this is going to turn out to be precisely true,

894
00:38:24,800 --> 00:38:26,722
but my intuitive picture of what's going on

895
00:38:26,722 --> 00:38:28,680
is that you're trying to fit the training data,

896
00:38:28,680 --> 00:38:30,038
so you have to memorize it.

897
00:38:30,038 --> 00:38:32,580
And you have to learn a really crazy function to memorize it.

898
00:38:32,580 --> 00:38:36,380
But then once you add more capacity, where now there

899
00:38:36,380 --> 00:38:39,500
are a lot of solutions that equally well memorize

900
00:38:39,500 --> 00:38:42,800
the data equally well, interpolate the data,

901
00:38:42,800 --> 00:38:46,020
then you can select within all of those set of solutions that

902
00:38:46,020 --> 00:38:48,960
fit the data the one that is the smoothest

903
00:38:48,960 --> 00:38:51,240
or has other nice properties.

904
00:38:51,240 --> 00:38:54,040
And the pressures that select within the set of things that

905
00:38:54,040 --> 00:38:55,800
fit the data the one that is the smoothest

906
00:38:55,800 --> 00:38:57,217
are sometimes called regularizers,

907
00:38:57,217 --> 00:38:59,380
or implicit regularizers, or inductive biases.

908
00:38:59,380 --> 00:39:01,140
And we'll talk more about those in a bit.

909
00:39:01,140 --> 00:39:03,060
It's like, for a while, you're just fitting the data.

910
00:39:03,060 --> 00:39:04,300
You have to find a crazy fit.

911
00:39:04,300 --> 00:39:06,040
And then once you have more capacity,

912
00:39:06,040 --> 00:39:08,880
you can search over that kind of degeneracy

913
00:39:08,880 --> 00:39:12,400
to find the one that is the best according to other criteria,

914
00:39:12,400 --> 00:39:13,380
like smoothness.

915
00:39:15,748 --> 00:39:17,040
Yeah, we'll go with a question.

916
00:39:17,040 --> 00:39:23,240
AUDIENCE: For [INAUDIBLE] descent, [INAUDIBLE]

917
00:39:23,240 --> 00:39:25,600
you should stop at the first place spot,

918
00:39:25,600 --> 00:39:30,160
or you should train like [INAUDIBLE] training

919
00:39:30,160 --> 00:39:32,640
until the second coverage [INAUDIBLE].

920
00:39:32,640 --> 00:39:34,240
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, so the question

921
00:39:34,240 --> 00:39:38,120
is, should you stop at the first minima of the double descent

922
00:39:38,120 --> 00:39:40,980
curve, where you have low risk on the population,

923
00:39:40,980 --> 00:39:43,560
or should you stop at-- or should you just train forever?

924
00:39:43,560 --> 00:39:47,210
And generally, I would say that it

925
00:39:47,210 --> 00:39:53,890
will depend on how much capacity you have available to you,

926
00:39:53,890 --> 00:39:56,610
because this is costly to move out in the x-axis.

927
00:39:56,610 --> 00:39:58,910
Capacity can be measured in parameters,

928
00:39:58,910 --> 00:40:00,590
but it could also be measured in flops.

929
00:40:00,590 --> 00:40:03,330
So there's kind of double descent also in compute time.

930
00:40:03,330 --> 00:40:08,390
And so it will depend on the cost-benefit analysis there.

931
00:40:08,390 --> 00:40:10,870
But generally, in deep learning, we're out in this regime.

932
00:40:10,870 --> 00:40:12,610
We're past the first peak, and we're

933
00:40:12,610 --> 00:40:14,810
in the part where you just train longer, and longer, and longer,

934
00:40:14,810 --> 00:40:16,150
and it smoothly will get better.

935
00:40:16,150 --> 00:40:19,070
That seems to be the empirical property we have.

936
00:40:19,070 --> 00:40:21,050
One more, and then I'll have to move on.

937
00:40:21,050 --> 00:40:25,050
AUDIENCE: What's the correlation between doing

938
00:40:25,050 --> 00:40:29,090
polynomial regression and training for a higher order

939
00:40:29,090 --> 00:40:29,650
polynomial?

940
00:40:29,650 --> 00:40:32,190
Is it similar to a Taylor series as that sum

941
00:40:32,190 --> 00:40:34,690
approaches infinity that you could approximate any arbitrary

942
00:40:34,690 --> 00:40:35,667
function?

943
00:40:35,667 --> 00:40:38,250
What's the relationship between that and a polynomial function

944
00:40:38,250 --> 00:40:39,227
in that [INAUDIBLE]?

945
00:40:39,227 --> 00:40:40,810
PHILLIP ISOLA: What's the relationship

946
00:40:40,810 --> 00:40:45,340
between polynomial regression and deep nets--

947
00:40:45,340 --> 00:40:50,820
so roughly, you can think of the last layer of an MLP

948
00:40:50,820 --> 00:40:54,140
as a linear combination of some basis functions,

949
00:40:54,140 --> 00:40:55,320
of some features.

950
00:40:55,320 --> 00:40:58,240
So every neuron on the previous layer is a feature of the data.

951
00:40:58,240 --> 00:41:02,122
And those features could be like polynomial functions of a scalar

952
00:41:02,122 --> 00:41:04,080
input, and that would be polynomial regression.

953
00:41:04,080 --> 00:41:06,700
Or they could be Fourier.

954
00:41:06,700 --> 00:41:09,380
They could be sines and cosines with different frequencies.

955
00:41:09,380 --> 00:41:11,560
And in deep learning, they'll be learned functions,

956
00:41:11,560 --> 00:41:14,060
but they have the capacity to learn those types of functions

957
00:41:14,060 --> 00:41:16,580
if those are the right ones to learn.

958
00:41:16,580 --> 00:41:21,020
So here's an empirical result of double descent.

959
00:41:21,020 --> 00:41:23,240
And this is training an MNIST classifier.

960
00:41:23,240 --> 00:41:24,840
And you can see this really happens.

961
00:41:24,840 --> 00:41:28,560
So the train error just always goes down as I add more weights.

962
00:41:28,560 --> 00:41:30,300
So I increase the width of my model.

963
00:41:30,300 --> 00:41:34,220
I get universal approximation, but test error spikes

964
00:41:34,220 --> 00:41:36,940
and then it goes back down.

965
00:41:36,940 --> 00:41:39,900
This stuff is a little finicky to actually observe in practice.

966
00:41:39,900 --> 00:41:42,510
So I actually tried to make my own example of this,

967
00:41:42,510 --> 00:41:43,860
and I couldn't get it to work.

968
00:41:43,860 --> 00:41:45,610
But if you read the double descent papers,

969
00:41:45,610 --> 00:41:48,670
you'll realize that it only really shows up

970
00:41:48,670 --> 00:41:49,730
in certain regimes.

971
00:41:49,730 --> 00:41:52,397
And you have to make sure not to have momentum in this and that,

972
00:41:52,397 --> 00:41:53,490
and weight decay.

973
00:41:53,490 --> 00:41:55,710
There's a lot of aspects of the optimization process

974
00:41:55,710 --> 00:41:58,550
that can make this disappear.

