00:42:20,890 --> 00:42:24,390
But I would say that, generally, all the tricks that we

987
00:42:24,390 --> 00:42:27,270
use in practice are just getting rid of that spike

988
00:42:27,270 --> 00:42:28,970
and pushing us more out to the right.

989
00:42:28,970 --> 00:42:31,550
So it's not like we're losing that benefit.

990
00:42:31,550 --> 00:42:33,030
We're reducing the negative effect

991
00:42:33,030 --> 00:42:35,610
of having this spike in the middle.

992
00:42:39,650 --> 00:42:42,290
So one of the interesting things that

993
00:42:42,290 --> 00:42:44,410
could help explain this phenomenon

994
00:42:44,410 --> 00:42:48,623
is that the more features that you have,

995
00:42:48,623 --> 00:42:50,290
that you're regressing on top of-- these

996
00:42:50,290 --> 00:42:54,715
could be features, like the hidden units of an MLP,

997
00:42:54,715 --> 00:42:57,090
or in this case, these are what are called random Fourier

998
00:42:57,090 --> 00:43:00,690
features-- like, random sines and cosines,

999
00:43:00,690 --> 00:43:04,370
random periodic functions to the data.

1000
00:43:04,370 --> 00:43:08,290
As I have more and more features, well--

1001
00:43:08,290 --> 00:43:09,990
at first, I underfit the data.

1002
00:43:09,990 --> 00:43:12,930
And then I start using all the features.

1003
00:43:12,930 --> 00:43:16,450
And my weights are assigned to those features spike

1004
00:43:16,450 --> 00:43:20,110
because I have to make a linear combination that fits the data.

1005
00:43:20,110 --> 00:43:23,170
I have to be using all of them.

1006
00:43:23,170 --> 00:43:26,170
But then what happens is, as I add more and more features,

1007
00:43:26,170 --> 00:43:28,958
the norm of the weight vector, or the parameter

1008
00:43:28,958 --> 00:43:31,250
norm-- the norm of that vector, the size of that vector

1009
00:43:31,250 --> 00:43:32,850
actually decays.

1010
00:43:32,850 --> 00:43:35,530
So I have more features, but the weights

1011
00:43:35,530 --> 00:43:37,850
I'm applying to those features are

1012
00:43:37,850 --> 00:43:39,460
getting smaller and smaller in terms

1013
00:43:39,460 --> 00:43:42,002
of their overall magnitude-- the norm of the entire parameter

1014
00:43:42,002 --> 00:43:42,660
vector.

1015
00:43:42,660 --> 00:43:46,940
So it's like, more features means lower norm parameter

1016
00:43:46,940 --> 00:43:49,300
solutions, so in some sense kind of smoother

1017
00:43:49,300 --> 00:43:51,020
in a particular sense.

1018
00:43:51,020 --> 00:43:53,912
And so this is just saying that counting number of parameters

1019
00:43:53,912 --> 00:43:55,120
is not the right thing to do.

1020
00:43:55,120 --> 00:43:57,980
It might be that the parameter norm or some other property

1021
00:43:57,980 --> 00:44:03,180
of the parameters is what is the more meaningful measure

1022
00:44:03,180 --> 00:44:06,200
of the complexity of the solution, or how wiggly it is,

1023
00:44:06,200 --> 00:44:08,420
or how much it deviates from a nice smooth fit

1024
00:44:08,420 --> 00:44:11,100
to the underlying trend.

1025
00:44:11,100 --> 00:44:11,920
Yeah, question?

