00:44:52,830 --> 00:44:55,230
the right notion of complexity according

1045
00:44:55,230 --> 00:44:57,470
to the classical theory that having

1046
00:44:57,470 --> 00:45:01,710
a more expressive, higher capacity hypothesis

1047
00:45:01,710 --> 00:45:03,850
space will cause overfitting.

1048
00:45:03,850 --> 00:45:06,000
So number of parameters, no.

1049
00:45:06,000 --> 00:45:08,250
Parameter norm-- maybe that seems a little bit better,

1050
00:45:08,250 --> 00:45:12,550
but it has its own flaws.

1051
00:45:12,550 --> 00:45:18,350
Just to emphasize this with maybe a really obvious example--

1052
00:45:18,350 --> 00:45:23,710
does the number of parameters a deep net has--

1053
00:45:23,710 --> 00:45:25,650
why would we actually expect that to matter?

1054
00:45:25,650 --> 00:45:28,990
So consider these two deep nets, f and g.

1055
00:45:28,990 --> 00:45:33,870
And f has a lot of parameters, a lot of edges.

1056
00:45:33,870 --> 00:45:36,953
And g has only a few parameters, a few edges.

1057
00:45:36,953 --> 00:45:38,870
And we're going to consider a new function, h,

1058
00:45:38,870 --> 00:45:41,970
which is just a combination of a tiny, tiny bit of f--

1059
00:45:41,970 --> 00:45:44,250
10 to the negative 100 worth of f.

1060
00:45:44,250 --> 00:45:49,250
And then almost entirely we're going to use g.

1061
00:45:49,250 --> 00:45:53,730
And now, we're going to ask how many parameters would it

1062
00:45:53,730 --> 00:45:59,370
take to fit h to some epsilon degree of approximation?

1063
00:45:59,370 --> 00:46:05,325
And the point here is that if I just fit h with g,

1064
00:46:05,325 --> 00:46:06,950
I would only be using a few parameters,

1065
00:46:06,950 --> 00:46:10,530
but get within epsilon is 10 to the negative 100

1066
00:46:10,530 --> 00:46:12,450
of the correct solution.

1067
00:46:12,450 --> 00:46:16,670
So it's kind of the count of parameters is not what matters.

1068
00:46:16,670 --> 00:46:18,810
Something about the size has to also

1069
00:46:18,810 --> 00:46:20,770
be what matters in terms of how well I'm

1070
00:46:20,770 --> 00:46:24,610
able to approximate different types of functions.

1071
00:46:27,250 --> 00:46:29,330
So parameter norm, size of the parameters

1072
00:46:29,330 --> 00:46:31,330
matters more than count.

1073
00:46:31,330 --> 00:46:34,220
But parameter norm is also not everything.

1074
00:46:38,080 --> 00:46:42,540
So let's try another idea from classical theory, which is--

1075
00:46:42,540 --> 00:46:44,020
OK, it's not number of parameters

1076
00:46:44,020 --> 00:46:46,980
that is the proper measure of the complexity

1077
00:46:46,980 --> 00:46:51,220
of a hypothesis space or a neural architecture.

1078
00:46:51,220 --> 00:46:54,380
How about the number of unique functions

1079
00:46:54,380 --> 00:46:57,860
in the hypothesis space?

1080
00:46:57,860 --> 00:47:01,520
It's another measure of capacity.

1081
00:47:01,520 --> 00:47:08,768
And this is related to a theory that Vapnik and Chervonenkis--

1082
00:47:08,768 --> 00:47:11,060
I don't know if I got the pronunciation right-- anyway,

1083
00:47:11,060 --> 00:47:11,960
VC theory.

1084
00:47:14,500 --> 00:47:16,365
So remember, generalization error

1085
00:47:16,365 --> 00:47:18,740
is the difference between the training error and the test

1086
00:47:18,740 --> 00:47:22,300
error, or the training error and the population error.

1087
00:47:22,300 --> 00:47:24,500
And the basic argument of this theory

1088
00:47:24,500 --> 00:47:28,060
says that if the number of training data

1089
00:47:28,060 --> 00:47:32,190
points I fit to is much larger than the number of functions

1090
00:47:32,190 --> 00:47:34,550
under consideration in my hypothesis space

1091
00:47:34,550 --> 00:47:38,630
I'm searching over, then you can guarantee to some degree

1092
00:47:38,630 --> 00:47:43,310
that the population error matches the training error.

1093
00:47:43,310 --> 00:47:46,450
So something about the size of the training data and the size--

1094
00:47:46,450 --> 00:47:49,750
the number of distinct functions that I'm searching over--

1095
00:47:49,750 --> 00:47:51,910
something about the ratio between these things.

1096
00:47:51,910 --> 00:47:55,012
So the intuition is like this.

1097
00:47:55,012 --> 00:47:57,470
So we could define something called a false positive, which

1098
00:47:57,470 --> 00:47:59,430
would be a function that fits the training data

1099
00:47:59,430 --> 00:48:02,870
but has high population risk--

1100
00:48:02,870 --> 00:48:04,330
a function that just gets lucky.

1101
00:48:04,330 --> 00:48:07,230
It fits but doesn't generalize.

1102
00:48:07,230 --> 00:48:09,690
So what is the chance of getting a false positive

1103
00:48:09,690 --> 00:48:13,710
if I'm searching over all of the different candidate

1104
00:48:13,710 --> 00:48:16,750
functions in my function class?

1105
00:48:16,750 --> 00:48:18,830
So the chance of getting a false positive

1106
00:48:18,830 --> 00:48:23,610
will be high if we just have more candidate functions.

1107
00:48:23,610 --> 00:48:27,130
Like, if I just have more candidate functions,

1108
00:48:27,130 --> 00:48:29,840
there's more random ones that will get lucky--

1109
00:48:29,840 --> 00:48:32,160
so that intuition.

1110
00:48:32,160 --> 00:48:37,460
And then it should be lower if I have more data points,

1111
00:48:37,460 --> 00:48:40,297
because every additional data point rules out

1112
00:48:40,297 --> 00:48:41,880
certain candidate functions from being

1113
00:48:41,880 --> 00:48:42,980
able to fit the data right.

1114
00:48:42,980 --> 00:48:44,688
Every new data point is a new constraint.

1115
00:48:44,688 --> 00:48:46,800
And it says there are fewer functions

1116
00:48:46,800 --> 00:48:49,240
in my hypothesis space, in my function

1117
00:48:49,240 --> 00:48:51,400
class that can fit the data.

1118
00:48:51,400 --> 00:48:53,760
So therefore, if the function class is small

1119
00:48:53,760 --> 00:48:56,320
and the training data is big, the chance of a false positive,

1120
00:48:56,320 --> 00:48:58,298
of just getting lucky, is low.

1121
00:48:58,298 --> 00:48:59,840
And therefore, I have a higher chance

1122
00:48:59,840 --> 00:49:02,140
that I really found a solution that generalizes.

1123
00:49:02,140 --> 00:49:03,820
That's kind of the rough argument.

1124
00:49:03,820 --> 00:49:05,900
And you can make this mathematically precise.

1125
00:49:08,960 --> 00:49:11,520
I have one more picture to make this argument just

1126
00:49:11,520 --> 00:49:13,420
in case that wasn't clear.

1127
00:49:13,420 --> 00:49:16,980
But let's imagine that I have this very simple setting.

1128
00:49:16,980 --> 00:49:20,320
I have a hypothesis space, which is a discrete set of candidate

1129
00:49:20,320 --> 00:49:21,920
functions, these circles.

1130
00:49:21,920 --> 00:49:24,320
So every circle is a candidate function.

1131
00:49:24,320 --> 00:49:26,840
The purple circles are the functions

1132
00:49:26,840 --> 00:49:29,020
that fit my training data.

1133
00:49:29,020 --> 00:49:31,292
And the true function is in green.

1134
00:49:31,292 --> 00:49:33,000
And we're going to make some assumptions.

1135
00:49:33,000 --> 00:49:34,260
We're going to assume that there's

1136
00:49:34,260 --> 00:49:35,510
no noise in the training data.

1137
00:49:35,510 --> 00:49:39,720
We're going to assume that the true function is in this set.

1138
00:49:39,720 --> 00:49:42,980
So it's not like I have a chance of finding it.

1139
00:49:42,980 --> 00:49:47,500
And we'll assume that my way of selecting from this set

1140
00:49:47,500 --> 00:49:50,680
is just I pick at random one of the purple points.

1141
00:49:50,680 --> 00:49:52,347
And the true function is also one of--

1142
00:49:52,347 --> 00:49:54,680
the true function also perfectly fits the training data.

1143
00:49:54,680 --> 00:49:57,900
So my optimization algorithm is just pick at random one

1144
00:49:57,900 --> 00:50:01,020
of the points that fits the training data.

1145
00:50:01,020 --> 00:50:05,780
So what happens as I increase the training data?

1146
00:50:05,780 --> 00:50:08,500
As I increase the training data, some of those purple points

1147
00:50:08,500 --> 00:50:10,880
turn off, because if I add a new data point,

1148
00:50:10,880 --> 00:50:14,140
some of the functions that fit the data previously no longer

1149
00:50:14,140 --> 00:50:16,560
will fit the new data because there's more constraints.

1150
00:50:16,560 --> 00:50:19,300
Every new data point adds new constraints

1151
00:50:19,300 --> 00:50:22,160
that you might violate.

1152
00:50:22,160 --> 00:50:23,940
So as I increase data, I strictly

1153
00:50:23,940 --> 00:50:27,390
will decrease, or at least not increase,

1154
00:50:27,390 --> 00:50:29,543
the number of these purple points.

1155
00:50:29,543 --> 00:50:31,210
So what happened is I add training data.

1156
00:50:31,210 --> 00:50:35,790
Right now, I have a 1 out of 6 chance of if I randomly

1157
00:50:35,790 --> 00:50:39,250
sample one of the purple or green points, 1 out of 6 times

1158
00:50:39,250 --> 00:50:40,950
I will get the true function.

1159
00:50:40,950 --> 00:50:44,790
But if I add more data now, it's 1 out of 4 times.

1160
00:50:44,790 --> 00:50:46,790
So adding data will increase my chance

1161
00:50:46,790 --> 00:50:50,973
of getting the true function under this procedure.

1162
00:50:50,973 --> 00:50:52,890
If I reduce the capacity, what does that mean?

1163
00:50:52,890 --> 00:50:54,670
That just means removing circles.

1164
00:50:54,670 --> 00:50:58,210
So the capacity is the number of functions in my function class.

1165
00:50:58,210 --> 00:51:00,750
If I just remove circles at random--

1166
00:51:00,750 --> 00:51:03,250
it's not necessarily how you would normally reduce capacity,

1167
00:51:03,250 --> 00:51:06,950
but this is one model of reducing capacity--

1168
00:51:06,950 --> 00:51:09,790
while still making sure that the true function is

1169
00:51:09,790 --> 00:51:13,790
within my hypothesis space, then I

1170
00:51:13,790 --> 00:51:16,950
will remove some of the purple spurious fits,

1171
00:51:16,950 --> 00:51:20,190
and I'll increase the probability of,

1172
00:51:20,190 --> 00:51:22,810
if I just select a random function, getting the right one.

1173
00:51:22,810 --> 00:51:25,720
So there's these two things that are happening.

1174
00:51:25,720 --> 00:51:27,920
Control capacity-- add more data.

1175
00:51:27,920 --> 00:51:30,543
They'll both end up increasing my probability

1176
00:51:30,543 --> 00:51:32,460
of getting the true function that generalizes.

1177
00:51:35,900 --> 00:51:41,140
So a few more technical details.

1178
00:51:41,140 --> 00:51:44,440
How do you count the number of functions in a function class?

1179
00:51:44,440 --> 00:51:46,440
I said we could have a discrete set of functions

1180
00:51:46,440 --> 00:51:47,920
under consideration.

1181
00:51:47,920 --> 00:51:50,460
But with a neural net, it's a continuous thing.

1182
00:51:50,460 --> 00:51:53,480
So it turns out that for the VC theory,

1183
00:51:53,480 --> 00:51:55,300
it suffices to do the following.

1184
00:51:55,300 --> 00:52:04,200
You simply count up how many discrete outputs your function

1185
00:52:04,200 --> 00:52:06,440
can apply to the training data.

1186
00:52:06,440 --> 00:52:10,360
So the simple version of this is I'm

1187
00:52:10,360 --> 00:52:14,420
going to assume that my function is outputting--

1188
00:52:14,420 --> 00:52:16,260
it's a binary output.

1189
00:52:16,260 --> 00:52:18,280
So it's going to output negative 1 or 1.

1190
00:52:18,280 --> 00:52:22,420
So it's like binary classifications of my data.

1191
00:52:22,420 --> 00:52:24,380
These are called dichotomies.

1192
00:52:24,380 --> 00:52:27,120
And let's say I'm doing an image classification system.

1193
00:52:27,120 --> 00:52:28,740
So I have some number of images.

1194
00:52:28,740 --> 00:52:32,380
And one unique dichotomy is plus 1 to the first image,

1195
00:52:32,380 --> 00:52:33,900
plus 1 to the next image, negative 1

1196
00:52:33,900 --> 00:52:36,700
to the thousandth image, and so forth.

1197
00:52:36,700 --> 00:52:40,420
And I can count up how many unique dichotomies

1198
00:52:40,420 --> 00:52:43,660
I can apply to my data, and that will be called

1199
00:52:43,660 --> 00:52:49,060
the VC dimension of that model.

1200
00:52:49,060 --> 00:52:49,840
Just one sec.

1201
00:52:49,840 --> 00:52:54,660
So VC dimension is the number of dichotomies over my n training

1202
00:52:54,660 --> 00:52:55,240
points.

1203
00:52:58,140 --> 00:53:02,020
And you can prove, under some assumptions, this generalization

1204
00:53:02,020 --> 00:53:05,340
bound that the population risk will

1205
00:53:05,340 --> 00:53:11,140
be bounded by something related to this square root of d over n.

1206
00:53:11,140 --> 00:53:14,980
So if my model class can represent more dichotomies,

1207
00:53:14,980 --> 00:53:19,980
can classify the training data in more different ways,

1208
00:53:19,980 --> 00:53:22,230
then my generalization bound will increase,

1209
00:53:22,230 --> 00:53:24,270
and I will not be able to say how well I'll

1210
00:53:24,270 --> 00:53:25,250
do on the test data.

1211
00:53:25,250 --> 00:53:27,990
It's like, more functions under consideration, so decreasing.

1212
00:53:27,990 --> 00:53:30,990
But as I add more data, n, then that

1213
00:53:30,990 --> 00:53:34,110
will rule out more and more of the spurious fits.

1214
00:53:34,110 --> 00:53:35,830
And so we have this ratio, and we

1215
00:53:35,830 --> 00:53:39,870
say that our population error will be bounded by this ratio.

1216
00:53:39,870 --> 00:53:43,270
So the cartoon argument is the same,

1217
00:53:43,270 --> 00:53:45,230
but you can prove under some conditions

1218
00:53:45,230 --> 00:53:49,390
that this is the exact bound that you get.

1219
00:53:49,390 --> 00:53:54,470
Let me finish this argument, and then we'll go a few questions.

1220
00:53:54,470 --> 00:53:59,630
So here's the interesting thing.

1221
00:53:59,630 --> 00:54:05,870
Neural nets can basically fit any dichotomy over regular data

1222
00:54:05,870 --> 00:54:06,450
sets.

1223
00:54:06,450 --> 00:54:08,310
So given a data set of cats versus dogs

1224
00:54:08,310 --> 00:54:11,670
I'm trying to classify, I can train the neural net,

1225
00:54:11,670 --> 00:54:13,610
and it will correctly classify cats and dogs.

1226
00:54:13,610 --> 00:54:15,330
But I can do the following thing.

1227
00:54:15,330 --> 00:54:18,210
I can randomly shuffle the labels.

1228
00:54:18,210 --> 00:54:20,680
So I take my data set where the cats are labeled cat

1229
00:54:20,680 --> 00:54:24,240
and dogs are labeled dog, and now I'll just

1230
00:54:24,240 --> 00:54:28,280
randomly permute the labels--

1231
00:54:28,280 --> 00:54:30,220
assign each image a random label--

1232
00:54:30,220 --> 00:54:32,480
just choose different random ordering.

1233
00:54:32,480 --> 00:54:36,060
So now, some dogs are labeled cats, some cats are labeled dog,

1234
00:54:36,060 --> 00:54:37,300
some cats are labeled cats.

1235
00:54:37,300 --> 00:54:39,360
It's completely random labels.

1236
00:54:39,360 --> 00:54:42,760
And it turns out that, in practice, the neural

1237
00:54:42,760 --> 00:54:45,560
nets we use can fit any random labeling.

1238
00:54:45,560 --> 00:54:48,480
So in other words, they can achieve any dichotomy.

1239
00:54:48,480 --> 00:54:51,640
So in other words, the VC dimension

1240
00:54:51,640 --> 00:54:56,560
is the total possible numbers of dichotomies of n points.

1241
00:54:56,560 --> 00:55:02,680
So how many different ways can I binarily classify n points?

1242
00:55:02,680 --> 00:55:07,000
2 to the n possible ways.

1243
00:55:07,000 --> 00:55:10,150
So every single point I can assign either a plus 1 or minus

1244
00:55:10,150 --> 00:55:12,850
1, and then I can do that for every other point-- for n

1245
00:55:12,850 --> 00:55:14,160
points.

1246
00:55:14,160 --> 00:55:18,515
And so the VC dimension d is equal to 2 to the n.

1247
00:55:18,515 --> 00:55:20,890
And the generalization bound is exponential in the number

1248
00:55:20,890 --> 00:55:22,515
of data training points-- is 2 to the n

1249
00:55:22,515 --> 00:55:24,690
over square root of that.

1250
00:55:24,690 --> 00:55:27,630
And it turns out that this is just extremely loose.

1251
00:55:27,630 --> 00:55:29,270
And in practice, it's just vacuous.

1252
00:55:29,270 --> 00:55:34,070
It says that my population error will be greater than 100%

1253
00:55:34,070 --> 00:55:38,370
if this is a classifier, or my population error

1254
00:55:38,370 --> 00:55:44,470
is less than 10 billion percent or something like this.

1255
00:55:44,470 --> 00:55:46,850
So as n becomes large, this scales exponentially,

1256
00:55:46,850 --> 00:55:48,910
and it becomes a vacuous bound.

1257
00:55:48,910 --> 00:55:54,170
It tells us nothing about how well the system will generalize.

1258
00:55:54,170 --> 00:56:01,730
So the VC theory doesn't explain deep learning.

1259
00:56:01,730 --> 00:56:04,930
It has the right, I think, paradigm

1260
00:56:04,930 --> 00:56:07,690
of thinking about the size of the hypothesis space, the number

1261
00:56:07,690 --> 00:56:10,890
of constraints, how many functions could fit the data

1262
00:56:10,890 --> 00:56:12,450
without generalizing well.

1263
00:56:12,450 --> 00:56:14,557
But the assumptions it makes are violated

1264
00:56:14,557 --> 00:56:16,390
and don't take into account the full picture

1265
00:56:16,390 --> 00:56:20,230
of all of the factors that are affecting deep learning.

1266
00:56:20,230 --> 00:56:22,690
One of the main ones is that, in deep learning,

1267
00:56:22,690 --> 00:56:25,290
we're not just picking a random hypothesis that fits the data.

1268
00:56:25,290 --> 00:56:26,870
We're using gradient descent, and that's

1269
00:56:26,870 --> 00:56:29,230
going to pick certain hypotheses and prefer certain hypotheses

1270
00:56:29,230 --> 00:56:29,910
over others.

1271
00:56:29,910 --> 00:56:32,490
Among all hypotheses that fit the data,

1272
00:56:32,490 --> 00:56:34,410
it might prefer simple ones over complex ones,

1273
00:56:34,410 --> 00:56:35,577
which we'll see in a minute.

1274
00:56:38,690 --> 00:56:40,550
And this is one of the optional readings.

1275
00:56:40,550 --> 00:56:44,270
There's a nice paper from a few years ago called "Understanding

1276
00:56:44,270 --> 00:56:46,750
Deep Learning Requires Rethinking Generalization."

1277
00:56:46,750 --> 00:56:48,430
It ran that experiment.

1278
00:56:48,430 --> 00:56:51,810
It took MNIST, and CIFAR, and some of the classic data sets,

1279
00:56:51,810 --> 00:56:53,850
and it randomly shuffled the labels.

1280
00:56:53,850 --> 00:56:56,670
And it showed that you can get 100% training accuracy

1281
00:56:56,670 --> 00:56:59,150
on any random shuffling of the labels.

1282
00:56:59,150 --> 00:57:03,510
But if you use the true labels, then your test accuracy

1283
00:57:03,510 --> 00:57:04,210
will be high.

1284
00:57:04,210 --> 00:57:07,790
But if you just randomly shuffle the labels,

1285
00:57:07,790 --> 00:57:10,330
now, there's no ability to generalize at all.

1286
00:57:10,330 --> 00:57:12,310
Your test accuracy will be low.

1287
00:57:12,310 --> 00:57:17,160
And VC theory would say that the generalization bound

1288
00:57:17,160 --> 00:57:18,300
is completely vacuous.

1289
00:57:18,300 --> 00:57:20,058
It doesn't tell us anything about how well

1290
00:57:20,058 --> 00:57:21,600
one or the other of these things will

1291
00:57:21,600 --> 00:57:22,660
generalize to the test data.

1292
00:57:22,660 --> 00:57:24,480
So there must be something more about what

1293
00:57:24,480 --> 00:57:27,260
neural nets are doing that's not explained by VC theory.

1294
00:57:30,260 --> 00:57:34,000
So neural nets can fit random labels,

1295
00:57:34,000 --> 00:57:38,320
yet when they're trained on real labels, they generalize.

1296
00:57:38,320 --> 00:57:41,520
This is the violation of classical theory.

1297
00:57:41,520 --> 00:57:44,392
Any questions there?

1298
00:57:44,392 --> 00:57:46,600
I think we had a few, so we can go back to the slides

1299
00:57:46,600 --> 00:57:49,560
where you had them if you still have them.

1300
00:57:49,560 --> 00:57:50,200
Yeah?

1301
00:57:50,200 --> 00:57:55,000
AUDIENCE: So a dichotomy is just [INAUDIBLE].

1302
00:57:55,000 --> 00:57:59,200
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, dichotomy is a binary labeling of all

1303
00:57:59,200 --> 00:58:02,880
of the training data points, so a vector

1304
00:58:02,880 --> 00:58:07,960
of assignments of the training points to 0 or 1--

1305
00:58:07,960 --> 00:58:08,980
negative 1 or 1.

1306
00:58:15,830 --> 00:58:19,410
So how should we measure model complexity

1307
00:58:19,410 --> 00:58:20,887
in the deep learning era?

1308
00:58:20,887 --> 00:58:22,970
Should it just be the number of distinct functions

1309
00:58:22,970 --> 00:58:24,530
the model can represent?

1310
00:58:24,530 --> 00:58:27,570
No, because deep nets can represent

1311
00:58:27,570 --> 00:58:30,410
a huge number of functions, and so many functions

1312
00:58:30,410 --> 00:58:32,730
that the classical bounds on--

1313
00:58:32,730 --> 00:58:35,810
that tell us how capacity relates to generalization

1314
00:58:35,810 --> 00:58:36,590
don't hold.

1315
00:58:36,590 --> 00:58:39,290
They would say that this is way too high capacity a model.

1316
00:58:39,290 --> 00:58:40,595
There must be some other thing.

1317
00:58:40,595 --> 00:58:41,970
There must be something else that

1318
00:58:41,970 --> 00:58:45,590
is helping us to find solutions that generalize

1319
00:58:45,590 --> 00:58:47,470
rather than solutions that don't generalize.

1320
00:58:51,090 --> 00:58:53,790
And unfortunately, I have to say that for deep learning,

1321
00:58:53,790 --> 00:58:55,543
we really don't know what it is.

1322
00:58:55,543 --> 00:58:56,710
It's still an open question.

1323
00:58:56,710 --> 00:58:57,710
There's a lot of papers.

1324
00:58:57,710 --> 00:58:59,450
There's a lot of new ideas on this.

1325
00:58:59,450 --> 00:59:01,410
I'm not saying that we don't know anything,

1326
00:59:01,410 --> 00:59:05,430
but a lot of the classical tools aren't working in this era.

1327
00:59:05,430 --> 00:59:10,490
And it's a great thing for us to try to make progress on.

1328
00:59:10,490 --> 00:59:15,270
So I'm going to use the final section to talk about some

1329
00:59:15,270 --> 00:59:17,590
of the inductive biases and pressures that

1330
00:59:17,590 --> 00:59:21,990
do lead deep nets to find solutions that generalize,

1331
00:59:21,990 --> 00:59:26,270
that give us some inkling of where the theory might go

1332
00:59:26,270 --> 00:59:27,530
and some practical tips.

1333
00:59:27,530 --> 00:59:30,370
But the recap so far is that, empirically,

1334
00:59:30,370 --> 00:59:32,430
deep nets generalize.

1335
00:59:32,430 --> 00:59:34,850
They can make reasonable predictions,

1336
00:59:34,850 --> 00:59:37,270
often accurate predictions on inputs that

1337
00:59:37,270 --> 00:59:38,730
are not in their training data.

1338
00:59:38,730 --> 00:59:42,230
And you can even show this for large language models

1339
00:59:42,230 --> 00:59:44,610
and things where they've been trained on so much data.

1340
00:59:44,610 --> 00:59:46,630
But you can just make a counting argument

1341
00:59:46,630 --> 00:59:50,250
that they still can't possibly be just memorizing.

1342
00:59:50,250 --> 00:59:54,190
They have to be doing some generalization.

1343
00:59:54,190 --> 00:59:56,990
And generalization, just from first principles,

1344
00:59:56,990 --> 00:59:58,930
requires some kind of inductive bias.

1345
00:59:58,930 --> 01:00:02,430
You have to have something that rules out the filing cabinet.

1346
01:00:02,430 --> 01:00:05,190
Only fitting the training data cannot fully explain

1347
01:00:05,190 --> 01:00:08,000
generalization, because you can fit the training data by just

1348
01:00:08,000 --> 01:00:11,360
memorizing the training points, but not having--

1349
01:00:11,360 --> 01:00:13,880
saying anything about the samples in between

1350
01:00:13,880 --> 01:00:18,350
the out-of-distribution samples in between the training points--

1351
01:00:18,350 --> 01:00:22,260
or sorry, the out-of-sample samples in the test set.

1352
01:00:25,320 --> 01:00:28,600
So the inductive biases can't really just

1353
01:00:28,600 --> 01:00:31,780
be about classical notions of model capacity,

1354
01:00:31,780 --> 01:00:34,220
like number of parameters or VC dimension,

1355
01:00:34,220 --> 01:00:36,480
which is number of functions that can be represented

1356
01:00:36,480 --> 01:00:38,200
by that hypothesis space.

1357
01:00:38,200 --> 01:00:41,160
So we need to understand what are the inductive biases,

1358
01:00:41,160 --> 01:00:43,600
and how do they relate to meaningful controls

1359
01:00:43,600 --> 01:00:45,780
on the expressivity and the capacity of the model?

1360
01:00:45,780 --> 01:00:48,680
How do they prefer simple or smooth solutions

1361
01:00:48,680 --> 01:00:51,720
over other solutions, and how do we measure that?

1362
01:00:51,720 --> 01:00:55,040
And this is very much open and ongoing research.

1363
01:00:55,040 --> 01:00:58,868
So deep nets must have some nice inductive biases.

1364
01:00:58,868 --> 01:01:01,160
They must control the complexity in ways we don't fully

1365
01:01:01,160 --> 01:01:03,280
know how to characterize.

1366
01:01:03,280 --> 01:01:04,840
So what are these inductive biases

1367
01:01:04,840 --> 01:01:07,730
that deep nets have that allow them to generalize?

1368
01:01:10,570 --> 01:01:13,215
So why do deep nets learn functions that generalize?

1369
01:01:13,215 --> 01:01:15,090
And the basic question is, other than fitting

1370
01:01:15,090 --> 01:01:16,290
the training data-- we understand that.

1371
01:01:16,290 --> 01:01:17,165
That's approximation.

1372
01:01:17,165 --> 01:01:19,540
We understand that fitting the training data is critical.

1373
01:01:19,540 --> 01:01:21,470
That's part of the recipe for generalization,

1374
01:01:21,470 --> 01:01:22,930
but there has to be something else.

1375
01:01:22,930 --> 01:01:24,230
Other than fitting the training data,

1376
01:01:24,230 --> 01:01:26,170
what are the other pressures that affect the solution

1377
01:01:26,170 --> 01:01:27,230
that deep nets arrive at?

1378
01:01:27,230 --> 01:01:29,050
There must be some other pressures

1379
01:01:29,050 --> 01:01:31,970
that affect which of the two solutions that both perfectly

1380
01:01:31,970 --> 01:01:34,942
fit the training data does the deep net choose?

1381
01:01:34,942 --> 01:01:36,650
And why does it choose the one that works

1382
01:01:36,650 --> 01:01:39,370
and not the filing cabinet one, which potentially it

1383
01:01:39,370 --> 01:01:41,430
would have the capacity to do in some cases?

1384
01:01:44,417 --> 01:01:46,750
So if we fit the training data, what's left to consider?

1385
01:01:46,750 --> 01:01:50,182
So this purple space is meant to be-- it's the same space as I

1386
01:01:50,182 --> 01:01:51,390
showed in purple dots before.

1387
01:01:51,390 --> 01:01:53,765
But now, I'm going to give it this name, "version space."

1388
01:01:53,765 --> 01:01:57,690
So the version space is the set of all hypotheses

1389
01:01:57,690 --> 01:02:01,770
in the hypothesis space that fit the training data.

1390
01:02:01,770 --> 01:02:04,110
So in neural nets, if I have an MLP,

1391
01:02:04,110 --> 01:02:08,450
the version space is the set of all settings of the parameters,

1392
01:02:08,450 --> 01:02:11,350
the weights and the biases, that perfectly interpolate,

1393
01:02:11,350 --> 01:02:15,270
that perfectly fit my training data that achieves zero training

1394
01:02:15,270 --> 01:02:16,150
error.

1395
01:02:16,150 --> 01:02:20,450
So some blob within the set of functions I'm searching over,

1396
01:02:20,450 --> 01:02:23,650
which is the set of weights and biases of the MLP,

1397
01:02:23,650 --> 01:02:24,650
if that is my model.

1398
01:02:28,150 --> 01:02:31,807
So basically, the point is that some points in this set

1399
01:02:31,807 --> 01:02:33,390
are bad and don't generalize, and some

1400
01:02:33,390 --> 01:02:34,515
are good and do generalize.

1401
01:02:34,515 --> 01:02:37,590
Why do deep nets find the ones that are good and do generalize

1402
01:02:37,590 --> 01:02:40,690
to some degree?

1403
01:02:40,690 --> 01:02:42,990
So these are all kind of ideas that are out there

1404
01:02:42,990 --> 01:02:47,710
but are not yet completely standard or proven.

1405
01:02:47,710 --> 01:02:50,850
But I think that they're fun to think about.

1406
01:02:50,850 --> 01:02:54,110
So the first one is the idea that there's

1407
01:02:54,110 --> 01:02:56,870
a simplicity bias in what's called the parameter function

1408
01:02:56,870 --> 01:02:58,950
map.

1409
01:02:58,950 --> 01:03:01,410
The parameter function map is-- remember

1410
01:03:01,410 --> 01:03:03,660
that we're trying to find functions that fit the data,

1411
01:03:03,660 --> 01:03:07,400
but we're trying to find them by searching over parametrization

1412
01:03:07,400 --> 01:03:08,238
of those functions.

1413
01:03:08,238 --> 01:03:10,280
So we have access to the parameters-- the weights

1414
01:03:10,280 --> 01:03:11,020
and biases.

1415
01:03:11,020 --> 01:03:13,520
But every setting of the weights and biases defines actually

1416
01:03:13,520 --> 01:03:16,080
some mathematical mapping f.

1417
01:03:16,080 --> 01:03:20,400
And there might be some parameter vectors--

1418
01:03:20,400 --> 01:03:23,840
two parameter vectors could define the same f.

1419
01:03:23,840 --> 01:03:30,042
Like, if I have a network which just adds a bias of one

1420
01:03:30,042 --> 01:03:32,000
on one layer and then subtracts the bias of one

1421
01:03:32,000 --> 01:03:34,800
on the next layer, and otherwise it is an identity,

1422
01:03:34,800 --> 01:03:39,000
well, that is equivalent to just a network that adds a bias of 0

1423
01:03:39,000 --> 01:03:41,420
and then adds a bias of 0.

1424
01:03:41,420 --> 01:03:43,020
So plus 1 minus 1 equals 0.

1425
01:03:43,020 --> 01:03:46,420
There are a multiplicity-- this is a many-to-one mapping.

1426
01:03:46,420 --> 01:03:49,003
There's a multiplicity of parameter vectors

1427
01:03:49,003 --> 01:03:50,420
that all map to the same function.

1428
01:03:50,420 --> 01:03:52,742
So the parameters and the function space

1429
01:03:52,742 --> 01:03:53,700
are not the same thing.

1430
01:03:53,700 --> 01:03:57,200
And we can look at the parameter function map.

1431
01:03:57,200 --> 01:04:00,130
And this is what was observed by these authors a few years ago

1432
01:04:00,130 --> 01:04:00,790
empirically.

1433
01:04:00,790 --> 01:04:04,170
They didn't prove this, but they observed it empirically.

1434
01:04:04,170 --> 01:04:08,410
If you just sample a random set of parameters

1435
01:04:08,410 --> 01:04:12,450
in an MLP or some other neural net models,

1436
01:04:12,450 --> 01:04:16,450
most of them will map to functions f

1437
01:04:16,450 --> 01:04:19,655
that are simple according to--

1438
01:04:19,655 --> 01:04:21,030
this is not number of parameters.

1439
01:04:21,030 --> 01:04:22,890
This is not number of distinct--

1440
01:04:22,890 --> 01:04:23,990
this is not VC dimension.

1441
01:04:23,990 --> 01:04:25,782
This is another one, Lempel-Ziv complexity.

1442
01:04:25,782 --> 01:04:28,890
It's like saying Lempel-Ziv is one of these compression

1443
01:04:28,890 --> 01:04:29,390
algorithms.

1444
01:04:29,390 --> 01:04:30,932
And I think this is saying like, if I

1445
01:04:30,932 --> 01:04:32,310
run that function f on some data,

1446
01:04:32,310 --> 01:04:34,410
how well can I compress it with this algorithm?

1447
01:04:34,410 --> 01:04:37,020
So another crazy complexity measure

1448
01:04:37,020 --> 01:04:38,770
that's hard to understand exactly how that

1449
01:04:38,770 --> 01:04:42,690
would relate to generalization, but they tried that one.

1450
01:04:42,690 --> 01:04:44,810
And so they're saying that--

1451
01:04:44,810 --> 01:04:47,360
this is like a probability distribution, I believe.

1452
01:04:47,360 --> 01:04:49,110
It's a little hard to interpret this plot,

1453
01:04:49,110 --> 01:04:54,170
but there is high probability of finding--

1454
01:04:54,170 --> 01:04:55,670
if I take a random parameter vector,

1455
01:04:55,670 --> 01:04:57,420
a random setting of the weights and biases

1456
01:04:57,420 --> 01:05:00,420
in a network, most random settings will be down here.

1457
01:05:00,420 --> 01:05:02,260
It'll be higher probability, and only a few

1458
01:05:02,260 --> 01:05:04,260
will be functions-- will define functions

1459
01:05:04,260 --> 01:05:07,500
that are really uncompressable and complex

1460
01:05:07,500 --> 01:05:09,700
in that particular sense.

1461
01:05:09,700 --> 01:05:13,060
So here's a way to look at-- an intuitive

1462
01:05:13,060 --> 01:05:14,460
way of looking at this.

1463
01:05:14,460 --> 01:05:17,300
So if we randomly pick a point in the parameter

1464
01:05:17,300 --> 01:05:20,140
space of the neural network, this

1465
01:05:20,140 --> 01:05:22,800
will tend to map towards simple functions.

1466
01:05:22,800 --> 01:05:25,100
So imagine that we're organizing this hypothesis space

1467
01:05:25,100 --> 01:05:26,960
by the simple functions.

1468
01:05:26,960 --> 01:05:29,780
The smooth compressible functions are down at the left,

1469
01:05:29,780 --> 01:05:32,060
and more complex, wiggly, incompressible functions

1470
01:05:32,060 --> 01:05:34,020
are up at the right.

1471
01:05:34,020 --> 01:05:37,188
So most random samples will be simple.

1472
01:05:37,188 --> 01:05:38,480
And then what does learning do?

1473
01:05:38,480 --> 01:05:40,462
Learning just moves our parameters

1474
01:05:40,462 --> 01:05:42,420
and, therefore, moves the functions they map to

1475
01:05:42,420 --> 01:05:44,440
toward the version space.

1476
01:05:44,440 --> 01:05:46,600
It moves you toward the space that fits the data.

1477
01:05:46,600 --> 01:05:48,500
And so we'll end up in this little section

1478
01:05:48,500 --> 01:05:51,420
where we're at the corner of the version space

1479
01:05:51,420 --> 01:05:53,460
where we have simple functions just by chance,

1480
01:05:53,460 --> 01:05:58,680
because most random initializations of my parameters

1481
01:05:58,680 --> 01:06:00,200
map to simple functions.

1482
01:06:00,200 --> 01:06:03,000
And most of the volume, as I walk through parameter space,

1483
01:06:03,000 --> 01:06:04,700
will also map to simple functions.

1484
01:06:04,700 --> 01:06:08,320
And so just by chance, we will find a solution

1485
01:06:08,320 --> 01:06:09,860
that is simple in that sense.

1486
01:06:14,193 --> 01:06:20,700
So I was involved in another paper that looked at that,

1487
01:06:20,700 --> 01:06:24,300
but looked particularly at a measure of simplicity,

1488
01:06:24,300 --> 01:06:26,432
which is the rank of the kernel.

1489
01:06:26,432 --> 01:06:28,140
So I'm going to describe what that means.

1490
01:06:28,140 --> 01:06:30,880
But we found that there's also this bias in the parameter

1491
01:06:30,880 --> 01:06:34,120
function map toward solutions that are

1492
01:06:34,120 --> 01:06:37,960
low rank in a certain sense.

1493
01:06:37,960 --> 01:06:45,440
So here's a MLP, W, ReLU, W. And here's a deeper MLP.

1494
01:06:45,440 --> 01:06:46,783
And one of the classic stories--

1495
01:06:46,783 --> 01:06:49,200
And we'll come back to this in the representation learning

1496
01:06:49,200 --> 01:06:49,700
lectures.

1497
01:06:49,700 --> 01:06:53,400
But one of the classical stories is that shallow nets--

1498
01:06:53,400 --> 01:06:56,730
let's say I'm trying to classify between 10 different classes

1499
01:06:56,730 --> 01:06:58,290
indicated by the colors.

1500
01:06:58,290 --> 01:07:00,712
I'm trying to take my data and map them

1501
01:07:00,712 --> 01:07:02,670
to a space where they're all linearly separable

1502
01:07:02,670 --> 01:07:03,630
so I can classify them.

1503
01:07:03,630 --> 01:07:07,410
So the classic story is that deeper nets with more parameters

1504
01:07:07,410 --> 01:07:09,050
have more capacity to organize the data

1505
01:07:09,050 --> 01:07:10,550
and separate all those classes.

1506
01:07:13,150 --> 01:07:14,770
So that's the picture here.

1507
01:07:14,770 --> 01:07:17,810
We get better linear separation of all these classes

1508
01:07:17,810 --> 01:07:20,290
at the top of the network.

1509
01:07:20,290 --> 01:07:23,490
Now, I'm going to characterize that distribution

1510
01:07:23,490 --> 01:07:26,970
at the top of the network with a kernel matrix, which

1511
01:07:26,970 --> 01:07:32,130
is going to be the similarity between every data

1512
01:07:32,130 --> 01:07:33,750
point and every other data point.

1513
01:07:33,750 --> 01:07:37,170
So this creates a matrix, a bivariate function.

1514
01:07:37,170 --> 01:07:41,570
And it's called the kernel of my embeddings

1515
01:07:41,570 --> 01:07:44,090
that the neural network arrives at.

1516
01:07:44,090 --> 01:07:48,450
So every row and column is--

1517
01:07:48,450 --> 01:07:50,130
every row is a data point and the column

1518
01:07:50,130 --> 01:07:51,230
is another data point.

1519
01:07:51,230 --> 01:07:54,220
And the blue value is the similarity

1520
01:07:54,220 --> 01:07:58,300
in the representational vectors at the output of that network.

1521
01:07:58,300 --> 01:08:00,720
So we'll see these kinds of kernels a few times.

1522
01:08:00,720 --> 01:08:03,300
This is a kernel that characterizes the representation

1523
01:08:03,300 --> 01:08:06,340
at the output of the network over the input data.

1524
01:08:06,340 --> 01:08:08,420
And if it's kind of block-structured

1525
01:08:08,420 --> 01:08:12,860
like it is here, that means that it's found clusters.

1526
01:08:12,860 --> 01:08:18,300
So that's like all the red points have grouped together

1527
01:08:18,300 --> 01:08:20,368
into one block, and all the purple points

1528
01:08:20,368 --> 01:08:21,160
into another block.

1529
01:08:21,160 --> 01:08:22,080
And that's good.

1530
01:08:22,080 --> 01:08:23,740
So that's kind of what we're looking

1531
01:08:23,740 --> 01:08:25,819
for if we want a good organization of my data

1532
01:08:25,819 --> 01:08:27,526
that's easy to classify.

1533
01:08:27,526 --> 01:08:28,359
It's very clustered.

1534
01:08:28,359 --> 01:08:32,380
It will look block-structured like this.

1535
01:08:32,380 --> 01:08:35,700
So the common understanding is that deeper nets

1536
01:08:35,700 --> 01:08:37,220
have greater capacity to organize

1537
01:08:37,220 --> 01:08:39,380
the data into this clustered structure that's

1538
01:08:39,380 --> 01:08:43,308
easy to then make a prediction on top of to classify.

1539
01:08:43,308 --> 01:08:45,100
But the interesting thing is if you get rid

1540
01:08:45,100 --> 01:08:47,939
of the nonlinearities, you actually get the same effect.

1541
01:08:47,939 --> 01:08:52,600
So just a linear network-- just a stack of linear layers also

1542
01:08:52,600 --> 01:08:56,439
will result in a deeper linear net--

1543
01:08:56,439 --> 01:09:00,960
results in more blocky kernels.

1544
01:09:00,960 --> 01:09:04,620
So we're going to characterize now the kernel with its rank.

1545
01:09:04,620 --> 01:09:07,960
So the rank of that matrix is how many

1546
01:09:07,960 --> 01:09:11,200
linearly independent rows and columns there are.

1547
01:09:11,200 --> 01:09:15,040
So blockier matrices have lower rank.

1548
01:09:15,040 --> 01:09:17,359
Lower rank is like, I have clustered

1549
01:09:17,359 --> 01:09:20,319
the data into a simpler format.

1550
01:09:20,319 --> 01:09:23,680
And that's useful for classification.

1551
01:09:23,680 --> 01:09:26,560
So you get the same phenomenon with deep linear nets.

1552
01:09:26,560 --> 01:09:29,760
And deep linear nets are equivalent to one

1553
01:09:29,760 --> 01:09:31,060
linear transformation.

1554
01:09:31,060 --> 01:09:32,920
So you can't explain this phenomena

1555
01:09:32,920 --> 01:09:38,479
as the deeper nets have more capacity to organize the data.

1556
01:09:38,479 --> 01:09:39,535
So what's going on?

1557
01:09:39,535 --> 01:09:41,160
The argument that we make in this paper

1558
01:09:41,160 --> 01:09:46,120
is that products of matrices tend to be low rank.

1559
01:09:46,120 --> 01:09:50,130
So again, it's like a parameter function map.

1560
01:09:50,130 --> 01:09:53,290
If I have a random set of weight matrices,

1561
01:09:53,290 --> 01:09:58,810
if I have more of them, just it will be higher probability

1562
01:09:58,810 --> 01:10:04,170
that I will output vectors whose kernel is lower rank.

1563
01:10:04,170 --> 01:10:07,010
So this is a property of random matrix theory

1564
01:10:07,010 --> 01:10:09,490
that products of random Gaussian matrices

1565
01:10:09,490 --> 01:10:12,570
will result in a low rank matrix.

1566
01:10:12,570 --> 01:10:17,690
Now, deep nets are not products of random matrices,

1567
01:10:17,690 --> 01:10:21,090
but the full argument is that if I'm looking at the weight

1568
01:10:21,090 --> 01:10:23,690
space of a neural network and I'm sampling a random point,

1569
01:10:23,690 --> 01:10:25,650
if I have a shallow network, then

1570
01:10:25,650 --> 01:10:29,370
most random points will be full rank.

1571
01:10:29,370 --> 01:10:31,650
If I have a deeper network, whether it's

1572
01:10:31,650 --> 01:10:34,730
a linear net or a nonlinear net, I

1573
01:10:34,730 --> 01:10:39,250
will tend to map to lower rank solutions just by chance.

1574
01:10:39,250 --> 01:10:41,845
Now, optimization will pick out which of the parameter

1575
01:10:41,845 --> 01:10:43,970
vectors actually is in the version space-- actually

1576
01:10:43,970 --> 01:10:45,130
fits the data.

1577
01:10:45,130 --> 01:10:47,600
But because most of the volume of parameter space is

1578
01:10:47,600 --> 01:10:49,860
mapping to low rank solutions--

1579
01:10:49,860 --> 01:10:55,030
low rank representations of the data, when the network is deep,

1580
01:10:55,030 --> 01:10:56,280
there'll be this kind of bias.

1581
01:10:56,280 --> 01:10:58,420
There'll be the bias that if I'm just

1582
01:10:58,420 --> 01:11:00,120
searching over some random location,

1583
01:11:00,120 --> 01:11:02,820
I'll tend to be more likely to find low rank solutions

1584
01:11:02,820 --> 01:11:04,220
than high rank solutions.

1585
01:11:04,220 --> 01:11:05,980
And empirically, this is what happens.

1586
01:11:05,980 --> 01:11:07,420
So a lot of these things are only

1587
01:11:07,420 --> 01:11:11,440
kind of provable and analyzable in linear models.

1588
01:11:11,440 --> 01:11:15,420
But then we can show empirically in real deep nets.

1589
01:11:15,420 --> 01:11:18,395
And what I'm showing here is the rank--

1590
01:11:18,395 --> 01:11:19,520
it's called effective rank.

1591
01:11:19,520 --> 01:11:21,437
It's just kind of like the rank of the matrix,

1592
01:11:21,437 --> 01:11:24,260
but a slightly different version of it.

1593
01:11:24,260 --> 01:11:29,178
So higher rank means you have these representations

1594
01:11:29,178 --> 01:11:30,720
of your data that are less clustered,

1595
01:11:30,720 --> 01:11:34,540
and lower rank means this more clustered-like structure.

1596
01:11:34,540 --> 01:11:37,738
And on the y-axis is the probability.

1597
01:11:37,738 --> 01:11:39,280
These are like probability densities.

1598
01:11:39,280 --> 01:11:41,020
So this is empirical PDFs--

1599
01:11:41,020 --> 01:11:45,060
Probability Densities-- of if I randomly

1600
01:11:45,060 --> 01:11:48,767
sample parameters of a neural network of different depths.

1601
01:11:48,767 --> 01:11:50,350
So if I randomly sample the parameters

1602
01:11:50,350 --> 01:11:56,150
of a depth-16 network, the functions that it maps to

1603
01:11:56,150 --> 01:12:01,190
will be these functions that map to low rank embeddings.

1604
01:12:01,190 --> 01:12:03,430
I know there's a lot of moving pieces here,

1605
01:12:03,430 --> 01:12:05,830
but basically deeper nets will be

1606
01:12:05,830 --> 01:12:10,510
biased toward functions that are simpler in the sense of having

1607
01:12:10,510 --> 01:12:12,130
lower rank structure.

1608
01:12:16,230 --> 01:12:18,310
So one more way of looking at it-- so this

1609
01:12:18,310 --> 01:12:26,310
is the rank which is in color and in height.

1610
01:12:26,310 --> 01:12:31,150
If I'm navigating two different dimensions of my hypothesis

1611
01:12:31,150 --> 01:12:33,810
space-- so my hypothesis space is parameterized by--

1612
01:12:33,810 --> 01:12:36,730
my parameter space has a million parameters, weights and biases,

1613
01:12:36,730 --> 01:12:38,510
but I'm going to look at just two of them.

1614
01:12:38,510 --> 01:12:42,830
And I'm going to chart out as I walk around that space what

1615
01:12:42,830 --> 01:12:44,050
is the effective rank.

1616
01:12:44,050 --> 01:12:47,530
And the point here is that as I add depth--

1617
01:12:47,530 --> 01:12:51,010
so with a single layer model, I will

1618
01:12:51,010 --> 01:12:56,050
have this kind of narrow ridge of low rank functions

1619
01:12:56,050 --> 01:12:58,390
as I navigate my parameter space.

1620
01:12:58,390 --> 01:13:01,110
But a two-layer model, I'll pull that ridge apart.

1621
01:13:01,110 --> 01:13:04,170
And so more volume of the parameter space

1622
01:13:04,170 --> 01:13:06,570
will map to low rank functions.

1623
01:13:06,570 --> 01:13:10,050
And if I combine this with optimization, I'll just--

1624
01:13:10,050 --> 01:13:12,830
more of the solutions will lie in the low rank space as well.

1625
01:13:20,325 --> 01:13:22,450
Yeah, one set of ideas is that deep nets are biased

1626
01:13:22,450 --> 01:13:24,750
toward simplicity according to Lempel-Ziv

1627
01:13:24,750 --> 01:13:27,730
or according to the rank of the functions that they learn just

1628
01:13:27,730 --> 01:13:30,650
because most parameterizations-- most settings of the parameters

1629
01:13:30,650 --> 01:13:33,050
map to these simple functions.

1630
01:13:33,050 --> 01:13:36,290
Another idea is that actually the optimization dynamics really

1631
01:13:36,290 --> 01:13:40,210
play a big role here, and that I'm not just

1632
01:13:40,210 --> 01:13:43,240
picking a random solution in my version space.

1633
01:13:43,240 --> 01:13:46,060
I'm actually using an optimizer, which will prefer some solutions

1634
01:13:46,060 --> 01:13:48,540
over other solutions.

1635
01:13:48,540 --> 01:13:52,820
And sometimes, I'll have explicit regularizers

1636
01:13:52,820 --> 01:13:55,860
that prefer simple solutions in my optimizer

1637
01:13:55,860 --> 01:13:57,608
or in my objective function.

1638
01:13:57,608 --> 01:13:59,400
And so one of those is called weight decay.

1639
01:13:59,400 --> 01:14:02,400
It's just saying that, as I optimize,

1640
01:14:02,400 --> 01:14:07,380
I'll tend to shrink the weights a little bit

1641
01:14:07,380 --> 01:14:09,160
on each update step.

1642
01:14:09,160 --> 01:14:11,540
And this tends to make weights that aren't actually

1643
01:14:11,540 --> 01:14:13,560
being used go to 0.

1644
01:14:13,560 --> 01:14:17,628
And so I'll end up learning a set of weights

1645
01:14:17,628 --> 01:14:19,420
of my neural network, which have lower norm

1646
01:14:19,420 --> 01:14:20,800
and are simple in that sense.

1647
01:14:23,340 --> 01:14:25,740
Often, we initialize our weights and biases

1648
01:14:25,740 --> 01:14:27,420
of our networks near 0.

1649
01:14:27,420 --> 01:14:30,980
And this acts like a bias toward finding solutions that have,

1650
01:14:30,980 --> 01:14:37,780
again, low norm solutions-- low norm parameter vectors.

1651
01:14:37,780 --> 01:14:39,540
And then there's a lot of interesting work

1652
01:14:39,540 --> 01:14:41,790
on the implicit biases of gradient descent

1653
01:14:41,790 --> 01:14:43,690
and stochastic gradient descent.

1654
01:14:43,690 --> 01:14:47,710
And so one idea here is that stochastic gradient descent--

1655
01:14:47,710 --> 01:14:49,670
well, gradient descent with a fixed step size

1656
01:14:49,670 --> 01:14:50,850
kind of descend down.

1657
01:14:50,850 --> 01:14:54,830
But let's say that I have a really, really sharp well

1658
01:14:54,830 --> 01:14:57,550
in my objective function, in my energy landscape.

1659
01:14:57,550 --> 01:15:00,130
Well, gradient descent can't go into a really sharp well

1660
01:15:00,130 --> 01:15:01,900
if I'm using fixed step sizes.

1661
01:15:01,900 --> 01:15:03,150
It'll just jump right over it.

1662
01:15:03,150 --> 01:15:05,258
That well will be too small.

1663
01:15:05,258 --> 01:15:07,550
So what gradient descent with a fixed step size will do

1664
01:15:07,550 --> 01:15:11,230
is it will tend to find these, what are called, flat minima.

1665
01:15:11,230 --> 01:15:14,510
So kind of basins that are low loss, but

1666
01:15:14,510 --> 01:15:19,590
are low loss over kind of a large region of the parameter

1667
01:15:19,590 --> 01:15:21,550
space that I'm searching over.

1668
01:15:21,550 --> 01:15:25,190
And these flat minima can be argued to generalize better

1669
01:15:25,190 --> 01:15:26,530
than these narrow minima.

1670
01:15:26,530 --> 01:15:29,968
The narrow minima might be more like just a weird solution that

1671
01:15:29,968 --> 01:15:31,510
got lucky, but the flat minima is one

1672
01:15:31,510 --> 01:15:33,610
that will generalize better.

1673
01:15:33,610 --> 01:15:36,110
So I'm not going to go into all the arguments for why that's

1674
01:15:36,110 --> 01:15:39,610
the case, but you can see these papers if you're interested.

1675
01:15:39,610 --> 01:15:43,030
But the optimizer is a critical part of the recipe too,

1676
01:15:43,030 --> 01:15:46,743
and it can affect the generalization properties.

1677
01:15:46,743 --> 01:15:48,410
But I think all of what I've said so far

1678
01:15:48,410 --> 01:15:50,210
is not actually the most important.

1679
01:15:50,210 --> 01:15:53,370
I think the most important is the following two slides-- is

1680
01:15:53,370 --> 01:15:55,450
the architectural symmetries.

1681
01:15:55,450 --> 01:15:57,523
And we saw these in ConvNets and graph nets,

1682
01:15:57,523 --> 01:15:59,690
and we'll see these in transformers and other models

1683
01:15:59,690 --> 01:16:00,190
too.

1684
01:16:02,770 --> 01:16:06,970
So our architectures are built to have certain invariances.

1685
01:16:06,970 --> 01:16:10,330
Like, if I'm pooling over channels

1686
01:16:10,330 --> 01:16:13,090
with a max pooling operation, I'll be invariant.

1687
01:16:13,090 --> 01:16:15,830
And my filters are like edge detectors.

1688
01:16:15,830 --> 01:16:19,890
I'll be invariant to the orientation of that bird's beak.

1689
01:16:19,890 --> 01:16:22,810
I'll fire regardless of what the orientation of that bird's beak

1690
01:16:22,810 --> 01:16:23,310
is.

1691
01:16:23,310 --> 01:16:26,570
So max pooling achieves this invariance over the dimension

1692
01:16:26,570 --> 01:16:27,710
we're maxing over.

1693
01:16:31,370 --> 01:16:33,570
Equivariances-- so graph nets and ConvNets

1694
01:16:33,570 --> 01:16:34,750
have these equivariances.

1695
01:16:34,750 --> 01:16:37,960
If I permute the labeling of the nodes,

1696
01:16:37,960 --> 01:16:41,340
then I will permute the predictions.

1697
01:16:41,340 --> 01:16:45,620
Translation equivariance-- if I have a filter,

1698
01:16:45,620 --> 01:16:49,340
and I slide it across the image, then

1699
01:16:49,340 --> 01:16:51,360
if I shift the image by some amount,

1700
01:16:51,360 --> 01:16:55,300
I'll shift my predictions by that same amount.

1701
01:16:55,300 --> 01:16:58,860
So these are symmetries that are kind of built

1702
01:16:58,860 --> 01:17:02,620
into our architectures that cause them to exhibit

1703
01:17:02,620 --> 01:17:07,100
out-of-sample generalization, because I know

1704
01:17:07,100 --> 01:17:10,660
if I just shift my image, it will respond

1705
01:17:10,660 --> 01:17:13,360
in the way it was trained to respond on the unshifted image.

1706
01:17:13,360 --> 01:17:16,397
That's what the symmetry gives you.

1707
01:17:16,397 --> 01:17:17,980
And then this kind of compositionality

1708
01:17:17,980 --> 01:17:19,730
I was mentioning before-- that the ConvNet

1709
01:17:19,730 --> 01:17:22,680
and a lot of architectures, they factor this input signal.

1710
01:17:22,680 --> 01:17:25,037
They maybe chop it up into little components.

1711
01:17:25,037 --> 01:17:27,120
They learn something about every little component,

1712
01:17:27,120 --> 01:17:29,340
but then the conjunction of components

1713
01:17:29,340 --> 01:17:30,993
is just given by something that's

1714
01:17:30,993 --> 01:17:32,160
defined by the architecture.

1715
01:17:32,160 --> 01:17:33,140
It's not learned.

1716
01:17:33,140 --> 01:17:35,990
The conjunction of the outputs of

1717
01:17:35,990 --> 01:17:39,750
these little patch-wise filters, patch-wise predictors

1718
01:17:39,750 --> 01:17:40,610
is not learned.

1719
01:17:40,610 --> 01:17:44,890
It's just you stitch them all back together.

1720
01:17:44,890 --> 01:17:48,110
So I think this is where really the real power

1721
01:17:48,110 --> 01:17:49,750
and the real explanation of why things

1722
01:17:49,750 --> 01:17:52,440
like ChatGPT, why they actually do

1723
01:17:52,440 --> 01:17:53,690
these feats of generalization.

1724
01:17:53,690 --> 01:17:56,350
It's not so much in my-- well, I don't know.

1725
01:17:56,350 --> 01:17:59,273
But I think it's a lot about it factorizes the world.

1726
01:17:59,273 --> 01:18:00,690
It carves the world at its joints.

1727
01:18:00,690 --> 01:18:03,610
It factors its world into parcels,

1728
01:18:03,610 --> 01:18:05,670
which are words, and sentences, and paragraphs.

1729
01:18:05,670 --> 01:18:07,630
And then compositions of these things

1730
01:18:07,630 --> 01:18:12,690
will be arrived at not from learning,

1731
01:18:12,690 --> 01:18:18,150
but from just how the architecture is built.

1732
01:18:18,150 --> 01:18:20,575
And then a lot of neural networks

1733
01:18:20,575 --> 01:18:22,450
have domain knowledge that's baked into them.

1734
01:18:22,450 --> 01:18:26,910
For example, Sarah gave the example of the NeRF model, which

1735
01:18:26,910 --> 01:18:29,550
is a neural architecture, but it uses equations

1736
01:18:29,550 --> 01:18:32,230
of perspective projection, and it uses

1737
01:18:32,230 --> 01:18:33,958
equations of light transport.

1738
01:18:33,958 --> 01:18:35,500
And these are built into the process,

1739
01:18:35,500 --> 01:18:38,080
and so it will generalize to new viewpoints

1740
01:18:38,080 --> 01:18:41,130
because it's using structures.

1741
01:18:41,130 --> 01:18:42,380
It's not just fitting to data.

1742
01:18:42,380 --> 01:18:44,760
It's data plus structure, data plus constraints.

1743
01:18:44,760 --> 01:18:49,160
And this is a drug discovery, drug prediction problem, where

1744
01:18:49,160 --> 01:18:50,300
they've baked in structure.

1745
01:18:50,300 --> 01:18:52,333
They know about how drugs are supposed

1746
01:18:52,333 --> 01:18:53,500
to interact with each other.

1747
01:18:53,500 --> 01:18:56,200
And the conjunction of fitting to data

1748
01:18:56,200 --> 01:18:57,800
under structural constraints is what

1749
01:18:57,800 --> 01:19:00,700
can allow you to generalize.

1750
01:19:00,700 --> 01:19:05,105
So I want to end with one final point, which is, well, maybe we

1751
01:19:05,105 --> 01:19:06,480
can actually go back, ultimately,

1752
01:19:06,480 --> 01:19:07,560
to this theory answer.

1753
01:19:07,560 --> 01:19:09,560
The theory answer is that the shortest

1754
01:19:09,560 --> 01:19:12,880
program that fits the data will be the one that generalizes.

1755
01:19:12,880 --> 01:19:14,762
I said it's intractable, but what if we say,

1756
01:19:14,762 --> 01:19:16,220
we don't have to find the shortest?

1757
01:19:16,220 --> 01:19:18,445
Finding the shortest is intractable.

1758
01:19:18,445 --> 01:19:20,820
Maybe we don't have to optimize for the shortest program,

1759
01:19:20,820 --> 01:19:22,920
but we can just say a short enough program that

1760
01:19:22,920 --> 01:19:25,520
fits the data will generalize.

1761
01:19:25,520 --> 01:19:28,840
And so this is something that I've just kind come back to

1762
01:19:28,840 --> 01:19:31,300
over the years.

1763
01:19:31,300 --> 01:19:33,360
This is a conversation I had with Ilya Sutskever,

1764
01:19:33,360 --> 01:19:37,620
who's well-known as one of the founders of OpenAI.

1765
01:19:37,620 --> 01:19:39,480
And so he had this thing he said,

1766
01:19:39,480 --> 01:19:41,560
which is deep-- this is not a direct quote.

1767
01:19:41,560 --> 01:19:42,920
This is just the rough memory.

1768
01:19:42,920 --> 01:19:45,620
"Deep nets generalize because they find small circuits that

1769
01:19:45,620 --> 01:19:47,210
fit the data."

1770
01:19:47,210 --> 01:19:48,960
And so at the time, I didn't quite get it.

1771
01:19:48,960 --> 01:19:50,240
I was like, small circuits?

1772
01:19:50,240 --> 01:19:50,880
What are you talking about?

1773
01:19:50,880 --> 01:19:51,672
Deep nets are huge.

1774
01:19:51,672 --> 01:19:54,100
Don't we need all of these inductive biases,

1775
01:19:54,100 --> 01:19:56,545
and regularizers, and simplicity biases?

1776
01:19:56,545 --> 01:19:58,920
Like, that seems critical to understanding deep learning.

1777
01:19:58,920 --> 01:20:00,128
And we need to bake those in.

1778
01:20:00,128 --> 01:20:02,860
We need to put those in our models.

1779
01:20:02,860 --> 01:20:04,560
And he said, "no, no, no, it's OK.

1780
01:20:04,560 --> 01:20:05,560
Deep nets are finite.

1781
01:20:05,560 --> 01:20:07,260
That's enough.

1782
01:20:07,260 --> 01:20:08,160
Finite is small.

1783
01:20:08,160 --> 01:20:11,100
Anything finite will look small if you have enough data."

1784
01:20:11,100 --> 01:20:12,860
And I think that was a deep insight

1785
01:20:12,860 --> 01:20:15,080
and maybe led to some of the success that he's had.

1786
01:20:15,080 --> 01:20:16,660
So I'll end there.

1787
01:20:16,660 --> 01:20:18,490
Thank you.
